WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.073
Welcome to our secondary education
professional learning

00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:08.789
workshop.

00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:12.497
Today we're going to explore Autistic
experience and what it

00:00:12.497 --> 00:00:16.298
means for access, belonging and
partnership in a secondary

00:00:16.298 --> 00:00:16.853
school.

00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:20.226
Keep the students you work with at the
centre of the day.

00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:23.911
We'll build our understanding, look
closely at everyday school

00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:27.169
routines and consider what we can change
in the environment, the

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:30.045
task and the support we offer.

00:00:30.045 --> 00:00:33.869
Our starting point is curiosity. We are
here to understand

00:00:33.869 --> 00:00:36.746
students and make participation more
workable.

00:00:36.884 --> 00:00:40.523
We value students as they are and support
their participation

00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:41.644
and belonging.

00:00:41.644 --> 00:00:45.710
You don't need to arrive with all the
answers. Bring your

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.644
questions, your experience and a
willingness to look again.

00:00:51.121 --> 00:00:56.043
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

00:00:56.044 --> 00:01:01.158
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

00:01:01.159 --> 00:01:05.698
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

00:01:05.699 --> 00:01:10.493
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

00:01:10.494 --> 00:01:13.689
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.780
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.920
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.880
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.320
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.520
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.530
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.640
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.270
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.140
Australia’s colonial history

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.850
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.280
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

00:02:00.681 --> 00:02:05.073
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

00:02:05.074 --> 00:02:07.599
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:14.901
Everyone is welcome here, and you are
welcome to be authentically yourself. Use

00:02:14.902 --> 00:02:20.066
the strategies that help you take part.
You can move, change position, write,

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:24.891
speak, listen or pass. If there is
something the delivery team can do to

00:02:24.892 --> 00:02:30.192
make participation more accessible, let us
know. Let's begin by getting to know

00:02:30.193 --> 00:02:35.425
the people around us. At your table, share
your first name, your role, and one

00:02:35.426 --> 00:02:40.183
hope you have for today. Keep the
introduction brief so everyone has an

00:02:40.184 --> 00:02:45.619
opportunity. You're also welcome simply to
listen. There is no need to disclose a

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.785
diagnosis or share private information
about yourself, a student or a school.

00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:55.950
What would make this learning useful in
your work? Take a moment to introduce

00:02:55.951 --> 00:02:56.968
yourselves now.

00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:09.456
Let's come back together. Keep that hope
in mind as we go. There will be

00:03:09.457 --> 00:03:14.540
opportunities to connect the content with
sensory experience, thinking, the school

00:03:14.541 --> 00:03:18.996
day, learning design and partnership. Your
questions are useful starting

00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:22.321
points, even when they don't have an
immediate answer.

00:03:23.841 --> 00:03:27.604
Before we get into the content, take a
moment to check the practical

00:03:27.605 --> 00:03:32.154
arrangements for your setting: the exits,
bathroom facilities, tea and coffee, and

00:03:32.155 --> 00:03:36.536
any quiet or lower-sensory space that is
available. Keep your Participant Guide

00:03:36.537 --> 00:03:40.917
and something to write with nearby. We'll
use the guide throughout the day, and

00:03:40.918 --> 00:03:45.074
there will be time to provide feedback
through the evaluation. You can also

00:03:45.075 --> 00:03:49.343
write questions down if speaking to the
group isn't your preferred way to ask

00:03:49.344 --> 00:03:53.725
them. Please be considerate with phone
sounds and calls, and follow the venue's

00:03:53.726 --> 00:03:58.107
arrangements for no smoking or vaping.
Attend to your own needs as we work. You

00:03:58.108 --> 00:04:02.151
don't need to wait for a scheduled break
to use a strategy that helps you

00:04:02.152 --> 00:04:05.913
participate. Confirm any local
arrangements with your delivery team.

00:04:07.441 --> 00:04:12.214
Turn to page four of your Participant
Guide. Bring two or three students to

00:04:12.215 --> 00:04:17.181
mind. Choose students whose experiences
differ in some way: perhaps their age,

00:04:17.182 --> 00:04:22.149
how they communicate, or what different
parts of school are like for them. Use

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:26.665
initials or a role description in your
notes. We won't need identifying

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:31.761
information. Begin with the whole person.
What interests them? What strengths do

00:04:31.762 --> 00:04:36.471
you notice? How do they connect with
people or with the things they enjoy?

00:04:36.472 --> 00:04:41.438
Then ask yourself: what am I here to
understand more deeply about their school

00:04:41.439 --> 00:04:46.148
experience? Keep that as an open question,
rather than deciding the answer

00:04:46.149 --> 00:04:50.793
now. This is private thinking first.
Sharing is optional. We'll return to

00:04:50.794 --> 00:04:55.696
these same students when we map the school
day, consider learning design, and

00:04:55.697 --> 00:05:00.792
reflect on what to carry forward. Give
yourself a moment to choose your students

00:05:00.793 --> 00:05:02.146
and make a few notes.

00:05:10.161 --> 00:05:14.883
Keep those notes available. You don't need
a complete picture yet. The gaps in

00:05:14.884 --> 00:05:19.238
what we know are part of the work. As we
move through the workshop, keep

00:05:19.239 --> 00:05:23.654
distinguishing what a student has
communicated, what you've observed, and

00:05:23.655 --> 00:05:26.412
what you'd still like to understand with
them.

00:05:27.921 --> 00:05:32.677
We're going to begin with student voices.
Listen to each speaker's own account, and

00:05:32.678 --> 00:05:37.261
allow it to stand on its own terms. You
don't need to diagnose, compare speakers

00:05:37.262 --> 00:05:41.554
with students you know, or reduce the
video to one lesson. Just notice what

00:05:41.555 --> 00:05:45.906
stays with you, and what you want to
understand more deeply. We'll have time

00:05:45.907 --> 00:05:47.470
to reflect after the video.

00:08:09.881 --> 00:08:13.781
Take a moment before moving into
discussion. Keep the speakers' own words

00:08:13.782 --> 00:08:16.380
and experiences at the centre of your
reflection.

00:08:19.401 --> 00:08:24.290
Stay on page four of your Participant
Guide. What did you notice? What has

00:08:24.291 --> 00:08:29.314
stayed with you? What would you like to
understand more deeply? Begin with a

00:08:29.315 --> 00:08:34.606
little private writing. Anchor your notes
in what the speakers said, rather than

00:08:34.607 --> 00:08:39.430
making an interpretation from their
appearance or behaviour. Then, if you

00:08:39.431 --> 00:08:44.387
wish, share one reflection with someone
nearby. Try to leave each speaker's

00:08:44.388 --> 00:08:49.612
account intact. We don't need to reach
agreement about a single message, and we

00:08:49.613 --> 00:08:54.502
don't need to compare a speaker with one
particular child. Make room for a

00:08:54.503 --> 00:08:59.459
question that remains open as well as
something that feels clear. Take some

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.335
time with those prompts now.

00:09:09.361 --> 00:09:14.177
Let's return. Keep one question from that
reflection where you can see it. We will

00:09:14.178 --> 00:09:18.340
come back to sensory experience, thinking,
communication and the school

00:09:18.341 --> 00:09:22.860
day. Some questions may need further
conversation through the program or your

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:27.261
local channels. Listening carefully
includes recognising what we still need

00:09:27.262 --> 00:09:27.796
to learn.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:33.968
Let's take a moment with the language on
this slide, because it gives us a shared

00:09:33.968 --> 00:09:38.208
starting point. Neurodiversity is the
whole picture. It includes everyone.

00:09:38.208 --> 00:09:42.914
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
brain patterns within that diversity, and

00:09:42.914 --> 00:09:47.271
autism is one expression of it. Here,
neurotypical describes the more common

00:09:47.271 --> 00:09:51.744
brain patterns. Neurodivergent describes
brain patterns that differ from those

00:09:51.744 --> 00:09:56.160
most common patterns. So both sit within
the wider picture of neurodiversity.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.517
Keeping those ideas clear helps us use the
language consistently through the

00:10:00.517 --> 00:10:04.990
day. Now, hold in mind the students you
were thinking about earlier. The words

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:09.521
can help us ask better questions about
their experience, but they won't tell us

00:10:09.521 --> 00:10:13.530
everything about any one of them. We still
need to know that student's

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:18.003
interests, their ways of communicating,
and what different parts of school are

00:10:18.003 --> 00:10:22.418
like for them. We'll also respect the
language people use for themselves. The

00:10:22.418 --> 00:10:26.834
purpose here is to support dignity and
curiosity. As we go, keep asking: does

00:10:26.834 --> 00:10:30.201
this language help me understand the
person in front of me?

00:10:31.721 --> 00:10:36.119
That brings us to what it means to be
neurodiversity affirming. The starting

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.224
point is that differences are not
deficits. There are different ways of

00:10:40.225 --> 00:10:44.799
knowing, doing, being and belonging, and
everyone has strengths and experiences

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.198
challenges. That means allowing for
different ways of thinking, learning and

00:10:49.199 --> 00:10:53.655
taking part. When we look at belonging,
for instance, we can stay open to the

00:10:53.656 --> 00:10:58.288
ways a student experiences connection,
rather than expecting it to look the same

00:10:58.289 --> 00:11:02.804
for everyone. In a secondary classroom,
it's easy for the things that need our

00:11:02.805 --> 00:11:07.262
immediate attention to fill the whole
picture. A difficult transition. A task

00:11:07.263 --> 00:11:11.484
that hasn't started. A moment when joining
the group has become too much.

00:11:11.485 --> 00:11:15.648
Those things matter. We need to understand
them and provide support. And

00:11:15.649 --> 00:11:20.282
we need to keep the rest of the person
visible. What interests them? What brings

00:11:20.283 --> 00:11:24.915
them joy? How do they connect? What can
they do that we might miss if we're only

00:11:24.916 --> 00:11:29.607
watching for difficulty? Take one of your
students now. What becomes more visible

00:11:29.608 --> 00:11:34.064
when you include their strengths and
interests in the picture? Just hold that

00:11:34.065 --> 00:11:35.296
thought for a moment.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.561
A strengths-based approach also takes
difficulty seriously. A student can have

00:11:48.562 --> 00:11:54.008
real strengths and still need substantial
support. Their capacity can change with

00:11:54.009 --> 00:11:59.591
the task, the environment and the day. So
our language needs to make room for both.

00:11:59.592 --> 00:12:04.356
We can describe what's becoming difficult,
notice what the situation is

00:12:04.357 --> 00:12:09.599
asking of the student, and consider what
support would help. We begin with the

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.390
person, and we stay curious about access.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.579
The social model helps us put that
curiosity to work. On this slide, we're

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:23.686
using an environment-first lens. We look
for barriers in the place, the task, the

00:12:23.687 --> 00:12:28.538
routine and the expectations. Then we ask
what can change around the student.

00:12:28.539 --> 00:12:33.453
Here's an imaginary classroom example. A
class is gathering on the floor for a

00:12:33.454 --> 00:12:38.368
story. The usual expectation is that
everyone sits close together, in the same

00:12:38.369 --> 00:12:43.539
space, for the whole reading. One student
finds that arrangement difficult. Before

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:48.263
deciding what the student needs to do
differently, we can look at what this

00:12:48.264 --> 00:12:53.434
routine asks of them. How crowded is the
space? What choices are there about where

00:12:53.435 --> 00:12:58.030
to sit? Is sitting in that particular
place necessary to take part in the

00:12:58.031 --> 00:13:03.201
story? We might explore another place to
sit, or a different way of joining. Those

00:13:03.202 --> 00:13:08.436
are possibilities to investigate with the
student. We haven't established what will

00:13:08.437 --> 00:13:13.351
help just by coming up with an option. The
next two steps on the slide matter:

00:13:13.352 --> 00:13:18.458
include the student's communication and
experience, and review whether the change

00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:23.565
makes participation more workable. What
can the student tell or show us about the

00:13:23.566 --> 00:13:28.480
change? Is it helping them take part?
Changing an environment won't make every

00:13:28.481 --> 00:13:33.140
difficulty disappear. It gives us
somewhere useful to begin, with barriers

00:13:33.141 --> 00:13:36.396
and supports that we can notice, discuss
and review.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.365
Let's widen that question to school
itself. You can use the space on page

00:13:42.366 --> 00:13:47.119
five of your Participant Guide as you
think. What is school for? Who is school

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.441
for? How do students learn? And whose ways
of communicating, moving and

00:13:51.442 --> 00:13:56.257
participating are treated as expected?
These are big questions. There isn't one

00:13:56.258 --> 00:14:01.135
correct answer we're trying to arrive at.
They're an invitation to look again at

00:14:01.136 --> 00:14:05.704
familiar routines and the assumptions
inside them. Think about a routine in

00:14:05.705 --> 00:14:10.582
your classroom: perhaps arriving in the
morning, joining a group or showing what

00:14:10.583 --> 00:14:15.213
you've learnt. What does that routine seem
to assume about how students will

00:14:15.214 --> 00:14:19.721
communicate or participate? What do we
notice and encourage? What might be

00:14:19.722 --> 00:14:24.352
harder to see? This is a chance to examine
the routine with curiosity. Every

00:14:24.353 --> 00:14:29.229
classroom has patterns that have become
familiar. Looking at them again can open

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:33.984
up choices. We can value the care and
effort that educators bring, while still

00:14:33.985 --> 00:14:38.430
asking whether a familiar expectation is
helping every student access the

00:14:38.431 --> 00:14:42.813
learning. For a moment, work quietly on
your own. Choose one of the four

00:14:42.814 --> 00:14:47.383
questions that catches your attention, and
jot down a thought or a question

00:14:47.384 --> 00:14:48.740
you'd like to explore.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:01.453
Hold on to the question you've chosen. Our
assumptions influence what we notice,

00:15:01.454 --> 00:15:05.849
what we reward and what we think needs to
change. We'll now take that wider

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:10.424
reflection into one specific routine, so
the question becomes something we can

00:15:10.425 --> 00:15:11.434
examine together.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:18.182
Now turn to a small group near you. To
begin, take the question about whose ways

00:15:18.183 --> 00:15:22.546
of communicating, moving and participating
are treated as expected.

00:15:22.547 --> 00:15:27.570
Choose one familiar routine or decision to
explore together. Keep it specific

00:15:27.571 --> 00:15:32.727
enough to picture. You might choose how
children join a whole-class discussion,

00:15:32.728 --> 00:15:37.686
for example. Talk about the routine and
the system, without naming students,

00:15:37.687 --> 00:15:42.975
colleagues or schools. Then work through
the prompts on screen. Which assumptions

00:15:42.976 --> 00:15:47.735
shape that routine? Who can access it
easily? Where might the environment

00:15:47.736 --> 00:15:52.627
create a barrier? How could curiosity
change the next response? If you find

00:15:52.628 --> 00:15:57.321
yourselves focusing mainly on what a
student does, come back to what the

00:15:57.322 --> 00:16:01.817
environment, task or expectation is
contributing. Before we come back

00:16:01.818 --> 00:16:06.577
together, choose one concise observation
to share, and one example of how

00:16:06.578 --> 00:16:11.404
curiosity could change your next response.
Keep the prompts in view as you

00:16:11.405 --> 00:16:12.726
talk. You can begin.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:25.526
Let's come back together. Finish the
thought you're on, and keep your notes.

00:16:25.527 --> 00:16:30.293
If you're sharing with the group, offer
one concise observation: one example

00:16:30.294 --> 00:16:35.314
where curiosity could change your next
response. Keep the example focused on the

00:16:35.315 --> 00:16:40.335
routine, the task or the environment. As
we close, keep that question about your

00:16:40.336 --> 00:16:45.102
next response with you. The shift we're
practising is from assumptions about

00:16:45.103 --> 00:16:49.678
students towards questions about access,
environment and learning design.

00:16:49.679 --> 00:16:54.318
Curiosity gives us time to look at the
task and the setting before placing

00:16:54.319 --> 00:16:59.339
another demand on a student. It helps us
identify something to explore, with the

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:04.360
student's experience still at the centre.
Keep your example available as we move

00:17:04.361 --> 00:17:04.551
on.

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:11.542
The Disability Standards for Education
2005 are part of the context for our work

00:17:11.543 --> 00:17:16.866
on access and adjustments. There are three
Australian Government Department of

00:17:16.867 --> 00:17:21.775
Education resources to be aware of
alongside this workshop. The first is

00:17:21.776 --> 00:17:27.307
Getting started. The second is Advocating
with and for your child: A workbook for

00:17:27.308 --> 00:17:32.631
parents and carers. That workbook is
written for families, so it is useful for

00:17:32.632 --> 00:17:38.163
educators to know what parents and carers
may be reading and working through. The

00:17:38.164 --> 00:17:43.142
third resource is Milestones and
transitions. The links are listed in the

00:17:43.143 --> 00:17:48.743
facilitator resources. Use the published
materials to follow up, and take detailed

00:17:48.744 --> 00:17:54.343
or school-specific questions through the
appropriate program or local channel. For

00:17:54.344 --> 00:17:59.391
our work today, keep the connection with
access in view: understanding the

00:17:59.392 --> 00:18:04.508
student, looking closely at the demands of
the setting, and considering the

00:18:04.509 --> 00:18:08.726
adjustments and support that make
participation more workable.

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:15.347
Autism can be understood as difference and
as a dynamic disability. Holding both

00:18:15.348 --> 00:18:20.260
ideas matters. Recognising difference and
strengths does not mean overlooking

00:18:20.261 --> 00:18:25.110
difficulty or the need for substantial
support. Access and support needs can

00:18:25.111 --> 00:18:30.153
change with the task, the environment,
relationships and the capacity available

00:18:30.154 --> 00:18:35.390
at that time. Something a student can do
in one setting, or on one day, may not be

00:18:35.391 --> 00:18:40.497
available in another. We need to look at
what is happening around the student as

00:18:40.498 --> 00:18:45.734
well as what the task is asking of them.
That takes us away from a fixed judgement

00:18:45.735 --> 00:18:50.583
about what someone can always do. It
invites a more useful question: what is

00:18:50.584 --> 00:18:55.432
making participation possible here, and
what support is needed now? Keep the

00:18:55.433 --> 00:19:00.281
individual in view. There isn't one
pattern that will explain every Autistic

00:19:00.282 --> 00:19:05.001
student's experience. Strengths,
difficulties and fluctuating capacity all

00:19:05.002 --> 00:19:09.915
belong in the picture we build with the
student and the people who know them.

00:19:11.441 --> 00:19:16.286
Here is where the day is taking us. We'll
develop a neurodiversity affirming

00:19:16.287 --> 00:19:21.003
approach, deepen our understanding of
Autistic experience, notice how that

00:19:21.004 --> 00:19:26.042
experience meets the school day, consider
accessible learning environments, and

00:19:26.043 --> 00:19:30.824
work in partnership. The first two
sessions build our shared understanding.

00:19:30.825 --> 00:19:36.057
Then we'll bring that understanding into
the rhythms of a secondary school day and

00:19:36.058 --> 00:19:41.226
into practical choices about design and
support. Belonging, capacity and joy stay

00:19:41.227 --> 00:19:45.878
with us throughout. The workshop schedule
allows for morning tea from ten

00:19:45.879 --> 00:19:50.789
forty-five to eleven, and lunch from
twelve thirty to one. Treat those as the

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:55.312
planned timings and follow the
arrangements for your session. Before we

00:19:55.313 --> 00:20:00.351
move on, turn to My starting point on page
five of your Participant Guide. Make

00:20:00.352 --> 00:20:05.585
a brief note of where you are beginning: a
question, a hope or an idea you want to

00:20:05.586 --> 00:20:10.625
examine. We will return to this in our
final reflection. Take a moment to write

00:20:10.626 --> 00:20:10.882
now.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:23.342
Keep that starting point. You don't have
to resolve it yet. Next we'll explore

00:20:23.343 --> 00:20:27.938
sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals, using the students you have in

00:20:27.939 --> 00:20:31.040
mind to connect the ideas with your own
school context.

00:20:37.561 --> 00:20:42.709
Let's return to the students you brought
to mind. In this session we'll explore

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:47.659
sensory processing, Autistic thinking and
internal signals. You can find the

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:52.346
session summary on page six of your
Participant Guide. We are building a

00:20:52.347 --> 00:20:57.363
fuller picture of each student. These
ideas give us areas for curiosity; they

00:20:57.364 --> 00:21:02.247
are not a checklist for deciding what a
student must be like. Keep noticing

00:21:02.248 --> 00:21:07.462
strengths and interests, the environment,
the demands of a task, and the support

00:21:07.463 --> 00:21:12.545
that may help. We'll look at these areas
separately so we can understand them,

00:21:12.546 --> 00:21:17.495
then begin putting them together again. In
the next session we'll carry that

00:21:17.496 --> 00:21:22.182
understanding into communication,
connection and the everyday rhythms of

00:21:22.183 --> 00:21:23.302
secondary school.

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:29.634
We'll start with sensory experience: how
the world arrives. Before we ask a

00:21:29.635 --> 00:21:34.559
student to join a conversation, begin a
task or make sense of an instruction,

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:39.549
there is already a sensory environment
around them. That environment can bring

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:44.410
pleasure, interest and connection, as well
as difficulty. Keep both in view.

00:21:44.411 --> 00:21:49.205
We're interested in sensory joy and
authentic expression alongside overload

00:21:49.206 --> 00:21:54.260
and barriers. As we work, remember that an
observation can open a question, but

00:21:54.261 --> 00:21:59.055
it cannot tell us everything about someone
else's internal experience. Stay

00:21:59.056 --> 00:22:01.712
curious about the person and the context.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.773
What do you understand by sensory
processing? Before we settle on a shared

00:22:07.774 --> 00:22:12.743
definition, take a moment to put it into
your own words. Think about what happens

00:22:12.744 --> 00:22:17.774
between information arriving through the
senses and the response you might notice.

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:31.412
For this workshop, sensory processing is
the way the brain receives, processes and

00:22:31.413 --> 00:22:36.887
responds to information from the senses.
There are three parts to hold together:

00:22:36.888 --> 00:22:41.946
receiving information, making sense of it,
and responding. We'll look more

00:22:41.947 --> 00:22:47.075
closely at that sequence in a moment.
First, let's widen the picture of the

00:22:47.076 --> 00:22:48.322
senses themselves.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:54.985
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell. This

00:22:54.986 --> 00:23:00.200
workshop uses eight. The other three are
proprioception, vestibular sense and

00:23:00.201 --> 00:23:05.757
interoception. Touch includes information
about texture, pressure and temperature.

00:23:05.758 --> 00:23:10.492
Vision gives information about things such
as colour, shape, depth and

00:23:10.493 --> 00:23:15.912
position. Gustatory and oral experience
includes taste, texture, temperature and

00:23:15.913 --> 00:23:20.784
the sensation of chewing. Hearing includes
pitch, loudness, distance and

00:23:20.785 --> 00:23:25.175
familiar sounds. Smell can also be closely
connected with memory.

00:23:25.176 --> 00:23:30.595
Proprioception gives us information about
where our body is in space, drawing on

00:23:30.596 --> 00:23:35.741
signals from our muscles and joints.
Pressure or movement may be something a

00:23:35.742 --> 00:23:40.682
student seeks for that input. The
vestibular sense relates to balance and

00:23:40.683 --> 00:23:45.553
movement, including the speed and
direction of head movement. Rocking or

00:23:45.554 --> 00:23:50.974
spinning may serve a regulating purpose
for some students. Interoception relates

00:23:50.975 --> 00:23:56.325
to signals from inside the body. We'll
return to it later in the session. These

00:23:56.326 --> 00:24:01.746
senses work together. A single classroom
experience can involve several at once.

00:24:01.747 --> 00:24:06.617
The three less familiar senses can be
especially useful to consider when

00:24:06.618 --> 00:24:11.695
something about a student's experience
isn't yet clear to us. Keep these as

00:24:11.696 --> 00:24:16.841
possibilities to explore, rather than
explanations we can assign by watching

00:24:16.842 --> 00:24:21.850
alone. Students will differ. There is also
an interoception webinar in the

00:24:21.851 --> 00:24:25.690
Positive Partnerships resources if you
want to follow up.

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:31.398
Here is the sequence again: sensory
information, processing, response.

00:24:31.399 --> 00:24:35.414
Imagine you're wearing a jumper and
becoming hot. Information about

00:24:35.415 --> 00:24:40.221
temperature arrives. You make sense of
that information, including what it means

00:24:40.222 --> 00:24:44.967
in relation to your experience, and you
might respond by taking the jumper off.

00:24:44.968 --> 00:24:49.105
In a classroom, this processing helps us
keep track of our bodies and

00:24:49.106 --> 00:24:53.730
surroundings, notice what matters and
direct attention. It is part of safety,

00:24:53.731 --> 00:24:58.659
learning and engagement. There is a great
deal happening before a response becomes

00:24:58.660 --> 00:25:03.466
visible to someone else. That is why we
need to look beyond the behaviour we can

00:25:03.467 --> 00:25:08.212
see. Sensory processing is not simply a
deliberate choice a student makes about

00:25:08.213 --> 00:25:12.411
how the world affects them. When we notice
a response, we can ask what

00:25:12.412 --> 00:25:16.914
information is arriving, what the
environment is asking of the student, and

00:25:16.915 --> 00:25:19.165
what we need to understand with them.

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:25.672
We're going to watch two short clips. The
first is an animation that explores

00:25:25.673 --> 00:25:30.731
sensory overwhelm through images and
sound. The next offers a personal account

00:25:30.732 --> 00:25:35.527
of sensory joy. Both belong in our
understanding of sensory experience. As

00:25:35.528 --> 00:25:40.191
you watch the animation, notice what it
draws your attention to. You may

00:25:40.192 --> 00:25:45.315
recognise something, or you may encounter
a portrayal that is unfamiliar. Leave

00:25:45.316 --> 00:25:50.308
room for either response, and remember
that no single portrayal can represent

00:25:50.309 --> 00:25:55.301
every Autistic person's experience. We'll
leave a quiet moment after the clip

00:25:55.302 --> 00:25:56.484
before continuing.

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:28.681
Take a moment with what you've seen and
heard. The animation gives us a way to

00:28:28.682 --> 00:28:33.578
consider sensory overwhelm, while each
person's experience remains their own.

00:28:33.579 --> 00:28:38.347
Keep any questions it raises with you.
We'll now make room for sensory joy.

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:46.900
In this next clip, we'll hear a personal
account of sensory joy. As you listen,

00:28:46.901 --> 00:28:52.086
consider what becomes visible when we
include pleasure, connection and the

00:28:52.087 --> 00:28:55.709
richness of sensory experience in our
understanding.

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:50.957
Sensory experience can include intensity,
depth and pleasure as well as difficulty.

00:29:50.958 --> 00:29:56.505
The same person can experience both joy
and overwhelm. For example, music may be

00:29:56.506 --> 00:30:01.632
a source of enjoyment for someone, while a
different sound or situation is

00:30:01.633 --> 00:30:06.477
difficult. That is an illustration, rather
than a conclusion about any

00:30:06.478 --> 00:30:11.744
individual speaker. Keep asking what
brings this particular student joy, and

00:30:11.745 --> 00:30:13.991
how school can make room for it.

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:20.575
Sensory joy and stimming belong in this
conversation. Stimming can be part of

00:30:20.576 --> 00:30:25.365
regulation, pleasure, connection and
authentic expression. Our task is to

00:30:25.366 --> 00:30:30.753
understand what it means for the student,
rather than treating it as disruption by

00:30:30.754 --> 00:30:34.944
default. Notice what brings joy. Recognise
the student's ways of

00:30:34.945 --> 00:30:40.000
regulating and expressing themselves.
Avoid making harmless stimming a target

00:30:40.001 --> 00:30:44.790
simply because it looks different from
what is expected. And ask what the

00:30:44.791 --> 00:30:49.713
student would like other people to
understand. Think about one student now.

00:30:49.714 --> 00:30:54.702
Where is sensory joy visible in their
school day? What opportunities do they

00:30:54.703 --> 00:30:59.625
have to enjoy, explore or regulate in ways
that work for them? Make a brief

00:30:59.626 --> 00:31:03.816
note of something you know, and something
you would like to ask.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:16.372
Keep those two things distinct: what you
already know from the student, and what

00:31:16.373 --> 00:31:20.331
you still need to explore. Sensory support
is about more than reducing

00:31:20.332 --> 00:31:24.519
difficulty. It can also make space for
pleasure and for ways of being that

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:25.724
matter to the person.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:31.726
This slide introduces four sensory
response patterns from occupational

00:31:31.727 --> 00:31:36.342
therapist Winnie Dunn's framework. You may
encounter this language in an

00:31:36.343 --> 00:31:41.479
occupational therapy report or a Sensory
Profile School Companion questionnaire.

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:46.551
The patterns are useful lenses, rather
than fixed types of person. Registration

00:31:46.552 --> 00:31:51.492
describes a pattern of noticing less
sensory information or missing cues that

00:31:51.493 --> 00:31:56.694
others notice. Seeking describes actively
looking for more input, perhaps through

00:31:56.695 --> 00:32:01.701
movement, touch or sound. Sensitivity
describes noticing sensory input readily

00:32:01.702 --> 00:32:06.383
and being affected by it, without
necessarily taking action to reduce it.

00:32:06.384 --> 00:32:11.454
Avoiding describes actively trying to
reduce input, perhaps by moving away from

00:32:11.455 --> 00:32:15.941
a crowd or covering the ears. The slide
also uses under-responsive and

00:32:15.942 --> 00:32:20.493
over-responsive language, and
distinguishes more passive responses from

00:32:20.494 --> 00:32:25.174
active ways of changing the input. Keep
that distinction connected to the

00:32:25.175 --> 00:32:29.726
student's experience, rather than treating
the labels as a judgement. A

00:32:29.727 --> 00:32:34.668
student may show different patterns for
different senses. Their responses may

00:32:34.669 --> 00:32:39.089
also change with the context, the load and
the day. We are looking at

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:44.226
nervous-system responses, not simply a
decision to cooperate or avoid a task. So

00:32:44.227 --> 00:32:48.843
use the framework to sharpen your
questions. What input is present? What

00:32:48.844 --> 00:32:53.394
has the student communicated? What pattern
might be worth exploring? An

00:32:53.395 --> 00:32:58.271
observation gives us a starting point, not
certainty about what is happening

00:32:58.272 --> 00:32:58.726
inside.

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:05.468
Let's apply that to a classroom routine.
Turn to the sensory activity on page

00:33:05.469 --> 00:33:10.835
seven of your Participant Guide, and work
in a small group. Choose one familiar

00:33:10.836 --> 00:33:15.995
routine that you can describe without
identifying a student or school. Start

00:33:15.996 --> 00:33:20.330
with the sensory demands of the
environment and the task, before

00:33:20.331 --> 00:33:25.628
discussing a student's response. What
might someone need to take in, filter or

00:33:25.629 --> 00:33:29.962
manage? You could consider sound,
lighting, movement, proximity,

00:33:29.963 --> 00:33:35.467
transitions or materials. These are areas
to investigate, not a universal list of

00:33:35.468 --> 00:33:40.833
triggers. Next, consider possible ways to
offer access or choice. How could the

00:33:40.834 --> 00:33:46.338
student communicate what works for them?
Treat each idea as a question to explore

00:33:46.339 --> 00:33:51.911
with the student, rather than a solution
you've already established. This is not a

00:33:51.912 --> 00:33:56.933
simulation of being Autistic. It is a
review of what the routine asks of a

00:33:56.934 --> 00:34:01.956
person. Choose the routine, map its
demands, then identify an option and a

00:34:01.957 --> 00:34:05.533
question you want to investigate. Begin
together now.

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:18.676
Let's come back. Look at the option and
question your group has recorded. What

00:34:18.677 --> 00:34:23.461
could you change, offer or investigate
before increasing the demands on a

00:34:23.462 --> 00:34:28.246
student? Keep the sequence in mind:
examine the environment and the task,

00:34:28.247 --> 00:34:33.563
include the student's communication, and
review what actually helps. The point is

00:34:33.564 --> 00:34:38.880
to develop a more precise question about
access, with room for different students

00:34:38.881 --> 00:34:40.540
to need different things.

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:46.700
We've been considering how the world
arrives through the senses. Now we'll

00:34:46.701 --> 00:34:51.004
turn to how the mind works with
information and where attention goes.

00:34:51.005 --> 00:34:56.004
Keep your students in mind as we explore
detail-first processing, deep focus and

00:34:56.005 --> 00:35:00.878
executive functioning. These concepts
won't describe every Autistic student in

00:35:00.879 --> 00:35:05.435
the same way. Use them to open inquiry,
and keep connecting them with the

00:35:05.436 --> 00:35:07.713
particular person, task and context.

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:14.612
A detail-first approach offers one way of
understanding Autistic thinking. Top-down

00:35:14.613 --> 00:35:19.657
processing uses expectations and the wider
picture to help filter information.

00:35:19.658 --> 00:35:24.571
Bottom-up processing begins with the
details and builds towards the whole. A

00:35:24.572 --> 00:35:29.943
detail-first account places more emphasis
on that detailed information arriving and

00:35:29.944 --> 00:35:35.054
being worked through. That can help us
consider both depth and load. Noticing a

00:35:35.055 --> 00:35:40.426
change, an inconsistency or a pattern can
be a strength. Processing a great deal of

00:35:40.427 --> 00:35:45.013
detail can also make a situation
demanding. Neither conclusion tells us

00:35:45.014 --> 00:35:50.058
exactly what a particular student is
experiencing. The strengths on this slide

00:35:50.059 --> 00:35:55.103
include pattern recognition, systems
thinking, deep memory, logical precision,

00:35:55.104 --> 00:35:59.820
honesty and directness, depth of
connection, and intense engagement. Take

00:35:59.821 --> 00:36:05.062
each seriously as something valuable in
itself. Strengths are part of a student's

00:36:05.063 --> 00:36:09.910
present identity and learning, rather than
compensation for a difficulty or

00:36:09.911 --> 00:36:14.234
only future potential. You might recognise
connections with sensory

00:36:14.235 --> 00:36:18.821
intensity, literal language or deep focus.
Keep these as connections to

00:36:18.822 --> 00:36:23.866
explore, rather than assuming a thinking
process from behaviour alone. Turn to

00:36:23.867 --> 00:36:29.107
Strengths I can see on page eight of your
Participant Guide. For the students you

00:36:29.108 --> 00:36:33.956
have in mind, note the strengths you
recognise. What evidence supports that

00:36:33.957 --> 00:36:38.935
understanding? What might you be missing
if your attention is mainly drawn to

00:36:38.936 --> 00:36:39.656
difficulty?

00:36:47.680 --> 00:36:52.213
Keep those strengths visible as we
continue. They can help us recognise what

00:36:52.214 --> 00:36:56.203
a student brings to learning and
connection, while still taking the

00:36:56.204 --> 00:36:57.896
support they need seriously.

00:36:59.401 --> 00:37:03.920
Think for a moment about your own
attention. Does it move across many

00:37:03.921 --> 00:37:08.772
topics, or settle deeply into one? That
question introduces monotropism: a

00:37:08.773 --> 00:37:13.957
framework for understanding attention
focused deeply on fewer things at a time.

00:37:13.958 --> 00:37:19.142
Dinah Murray, Mike Lesser and Wenn Lawson
developed the framework, and Autistic

00:37:19.143 --> 00:37:24.193
community voices have continued to extend
it. One image is a torch beam and a

00:37:24.194 --> 00:37:29.644
floodlight. A floodlight spreads across a
wide area; a torch illuminates a narrower

00:37:29.645 --> 00:37:34.629
area intensely. Another is a river with
many channels compared with one deep

00:37:34.630 --> 00:37:39.481
channel. These are ways to picture
different patterns of attention, rather

00:37:39.482 --> 00:37:44.599
than a test for sorting people. Deep
interests can provide a strong route into

00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:49.917
learning, memory and joy. Flow, that full
absorption in an activity, is something

00:37:49.918 --> 00:37:55.168
worth protecting. It is more useful to
recognise that engagement than dismiss it

00:37:55.169 --> 00:38:00.353
as an obsession. Now picture a student
absorbed in a construction task, drawing

00:38:00.354 --> 00:38:05.205
or researching space. Asking them to stop
when the bell goes may require a

00:38:05.206 --> 00:38:10.589
substantial shift. Tendril theory offers
another image: attention reaching into an

00:38:10.590 --> 00:38:15.840
activity, then needing time to withdraw
before moving elsewhere. Transitions can

00:38:15.841 --> 00:38:21.291
have a real cost. Warning and scaffolding
may help make that shift more manageable.

00:38:21.292 --> 00:38:26.010
Keep asking what support fits this student
and this moment. Detail-first

00:38:26.011 --> 00:38:30.796
processing helps us consider how
information arrives; deep focus helps us

00:38:30.797 --> 00:38:32.790
consider where attention goes.

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:39.655
Executive functioning gives us another set
of questions about the demands of the

00:38:39.656 --> 00:38:44.923
school day. Let's name the ten areas on
the slide. Planning is working out what

00:38:44.924 --> 00:38:49.854
needs to happen. Organisation is arranging
materials and information. Time

00:38:49.855 --> 00:38:54.447
management involves judging and using
time. Working memory helps hold

00:38:54.448 --> 00:38:59.445
information in mind while we use it.
Metacognition means thinking about our

00:38:59.446 --> 00:39:04.308
own thinking. Emotional regulation
concerns managing emotional responses.

00:39:04.309 --> 00:39:09.374
Flexibility involves adapting when
something changes. Attention is directing

00:39:09.375 --> 00:39:14.169
and sustaining focus. Task initiation is
getting started. Persistence is

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:19.572
continuing with a task. Now consider an
ordinary classroom activity. What does it

00:39:19.573 --> 00:39:24.908
ask a student to plan, start, remember,
sequence, organise, switch and complete?

00:39:24.909 --> 00:39:29.839
A seemingly simple instruction can contain
several demands. Interruptions,

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:34.770
timing and the way instructions are
presented can change what is required.

00:39:34.771 --> 00:39:40.240
These terms are lenses for inquiry, not a
checklist to apply to a student. A delay

00:39:40.241 --> 00:39:45.644
in starting does not tell us, by itself,
that someone lacks effort or motivation.

00:39:45.645 --> 00:39:50.845
Useful support responds to the specific
demand in the specific context. As you

00:39:50.846 --> 00:39:56.248
look across the ten areas, consider where
each appears in the school day for your

00:39:56.249 --> 00:40:01.111
students, and where task design, timing or
support might make access more

00:40:01.112 --> 00:40:01.719
workable.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:07.564
Let's take getting started as one example.
The slide connects it with

00:40:07.565 --> 00:40:12.461
detail-first processing: starting can be
hard when the whole task is not clear

00:40:12.462 --> 00:40:17.104
yet. Some students may not be able to
identify the first step they need to

00:40:17.105 --> 00:40:21.302
take. That gives us a more useful question
than simply repeating an

00:40:21.303 --> 00:40:26.453
instruction to begin. Is the task clear?
Is its purpose and shape available to the

00:40:26.454 --> 00:40:31.223
student? Can they identify a first step?
Those questions don't establish the

00:40:31.224 --> 00:40:35.485
reason for a particular student's
difficulty. They give us something

00:40:35.486 --> 00:40:40.191
specific to check with them. Support can
then respond to the demand we have

00:40:40.192 --> 00:40:43.689
understood, rather than an assumption
about willingness.

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:49.731
Switching focus gives us a second example.
It can be difficult for some

00:40:49.732 --> 00:40:54.716
students to stop what they are doing and
move their attention elsewhere. Think

00:40:54.717 --> 00:40:59.961
back to the student deeply absorbed in an
activity when recess begins. Transitions

00:40:59.962 --> 00:41:04.816
and unexpected interruptions can be
significant because attention is already

00:41:04.817 --> 00:41:09.996
deeply invested. The slide uses the image
of tunnel vision for the mind. It helps

00:41:09.997 --> 00:41:14.657
us consider the effort involved in
disengaging and redirecting attention.

00:41:14.658 --> 00:41:19.707
Keep the question practical: what is the
transition asking of this student, and

00:41:19.708 --> 00:41:24.757
what warning or scaffolding could make it
more manageable? A timetable tells us

00:41:24.758 --> 00:41:29.484
when the next activity starts. It doesn't,
by itself, tell us what someone

00:41:29.485 --> 00:41:31.685
needs in order to make that shift.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:37.905
A young person's account of deep focus and
transitions is planned for this point

00:41:37.906 --> 00:41:42.373
in the workshop, but the clip is not
included in the supplied materials yet.

00:41:42.374 --> 00:41:46.781
Hearing directly from Autistic people
remains central to this learning. The

00:41:46.782 --> 00:41:51.487
frameworks we've introduced can help us
form questions; they cannot stand in for

00:41:51.488 --> 00:41:55.776
a person's own account. Keep that
distinction as we move into discussion.

00:41:55.777 --> 00:42:00.125
What do you know from the students
themselves about their interests, their

00:42:00.126 --> 00:42:04.653
focus and the support they find useful?
Where do you still need to listen and

00:42:04.654 --> 00:42:05.010
learn?

00:42:06.520 --> 00:42:11.655
Turn to the thinking discussion on page
eight, and work with a partner. Hold your

00:42:11.656 --> 00:42:16.792
students in mind as you explore the four
questions on screen. What deep interests

00:42:16.793 --> 00:42:21.222
do they have? What do you notice when they
are fully engaged, and does

00:42:21.223 --> 00:42:26.166
engagement look the same for each of them?
When in the school day do they have

00:42:26.167 --> 00:42:30.788
trouble getting started or shifting
attention? And have you observed them

00:42:30.789 --> 00:42:35.732
fully absorbed in something at school?
What were they doing? Include strengths

00:42:35.733 --> 00:42:40.676
and flow alongside the parts that are
difficult. Separate what a task requires

00:42:40.677 --> 00:42:45.684
from what you observe a student doing, and
leave their internal experience open

00:42:45.685 --> 00:42:50.885
unless they have communicated it. If you
want to extend the discussion, choose one

00:42:50.886 --> 00:42:56.149
task and describe it neutrally. List what
it asks someone to notice, start, hold in

00:42:56.150 --> 00:43:01.093
mind, switch, sequence and complete. Mark
where the environment or instruction

00:43:01.094 --> 00:43:05.908
might reduce unnecessary demand, then
write one question to explore with the

00:43:05.909 --> 00:43:10.339
student or team. Begin with the four
questions, and leave time to note

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:12.650
something you want to carry forward.

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:25.291
Let's come back together. If you're
sharing, choose one brief observation or

00:43:25.292 --> 00:43:29.658
question from your discussion. Keep it
connected with a specific student

00:43:29.659 --> 00:43:34.516
experience or task, without identifying
the student. The purpose is to move from

00:43:34.517 --> 00:43:38.943
understanding a concept to recognising
something worth exploring in daily

00:43:38.944 --> 00:43:43.556
learning. Deep interests, starting,
shifting and flow can all help us notice

00:43:43.557 --> 00:43:48.168
more carefully. We can work with the
student's attention and strengths while

00:43:48.169 --> 00:43:53.088
considering the support they need. Keep
those notes for our next session, when we

00:43:53.089 --> 00:43:56.716
connect this thinking with communication
and the school day.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:02.853
The final part of this session turns
inward. We named interoception among the

00:44:02.854 --> 00:44:07.467
eight senses; now we'll return to it more
closely. What is happening inside a

00:44:07.468 --> 00:44:12.202
person may not be visible to an observer.
A student can be having an experience

00:44:12.203 --> 00:44:16.695
that we do not recognise from the surface.
Keep that in mind as we consider

00:44:16.696 --> 00:44:20.762
body signals, emotions and the ways we
build understanding together.

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:27.231
Interoception is about understanding our
body's internal signals. It helps us

00:44:27.232 --> 00:44:32.575
answer the question, how am I doing? Am I
hot or cold? Am I hungry or thirsty? Do I

00:44:32.576 --> 00:44:37.201
need the bathroom? Am I tired? These
signals also contribute to noticing

00:44:37.202 --> 00:44:41.958
changes as we become upset and to
regulation. The other term on this slide

00:44:41.959 --> 00:44:46.845
is alexithymia: difficulty identifying and
describing emotions and feelings.

00:44:46.846 --> 00:44:51.863
Keep that definition clear. Identifying
what is happening and finding words to

00:44:51.864 --> 00:44:56.555
describe it can be difficult. The
questions on screen invite us to notice

00:44:56.556 --> 00:45:01.703
how much interpretation can be involved.
Am I excited or am I anxious? Is crying

00:45:01.704 --> 00:45:06.394
always sadness? What is my friend feeling?
We shouldn't assume the answer

00:45:06.395 --> 00:45:11.086
from outward behaviour. Crying alone, for
example, does not establish one

00:45:11.087 --> 00:45:16.039
particular feeling. And an apparently calm
surface doesn't tell us everything

00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:21.121
about what is happening inside. These
terms help us recognise why understanding

00:45:21.122 --> 00:45:26.009
internal experience matters. They are a
reason to stay open to the student's

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:31.026
communication and to build understanding
together, rather than to decide their

00:45:31.027 --> 00:45:32.590
internal state for them.

00:45:34.120 --> 00:45:38.815
Sometimes a student experiences a build-up
inside, while the surface gives

00:45:38.816 --> 00:45:43.832
us little indication. If we wait until
distress is obvious, we may miss earlier

00:45:43.833 --> 00:45:48.721
opportunities to respond. The more we
understand a student's early signs, the

00:45:48.722 --> 00:45:53.030
better placed we are to recognise when
support might be useful. That

00:45:53.031 --> 00:45:58.305
understanding needs to be built with each
student, their family and other staff. No

00:45:58.306 --> 00:46:03.065
one person's observation gives us the
whole picture. Think of a shared map:

00:46:03.066 --> 00:46:07.696
what the student communicates, what
different people notice, what remains

00:46:07.697 --> 00:46:12.456
uncertain, and how people can respond
consistently. The map needs to remain

00:46:12.457 --> 00:46:16.959
open to revision as circumstances and
capacity change. An analogy or an

00:46:16.960 --> 00:46:21.075
observation can help start a conversation.
It doesn't replace the

00:46:21.076 --> 00:46:26.286
student's own communication. Keep asking
how the student can contribute, including

00:46:26.287 --> 00:46:31.432
in ways other than a spoken explanation,
and what the environment can do with the

00:46:31.433 --> 00:46:32.911
information they share.

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:39.645
Turn to Reading the signs on page nine of
your Participant Guide, and work with a

00:46:39.646 --> 00:46:44.396
partner. For the students you have in
mind, what signs might you notice at

00:46:44.397 --> 00:46:49.016
school before they become overwhelmed? Are
those signs the same for each

00:46:49.017 --> 00:46:53.702
student? Use initials or roles in your
notes, and distinguish what you've

00:46:53.703 --> 00:46:58.713
observed from what you think it might
mean. Then consider what would be useful

00:46:58.714 --> 00:47:03.594
for other staff and families to know. What
have they noticed that could help

00:47:03.595 --> 00:47:08.800
you understand the picture? How could the
student communicate their capacity, and

00:47:08.801 --> 00:47:13.486
what could the environment do in response?
You don't need to settle on an

00:47:13.487 --> 00:47:18.171
explanation for every sign. Aim to
identify something useful to share and

00:47:18.172 --> 00:47:22.987
something you still need to explore
together. Take a few minutes with those

00:47:22.988 --> 00:47:23.898
questions now.

00:47:31.920 --> 00:47:36.669
Let's return. Keep one point that would be
useful to discuss with the student,

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:41.235
their family or other staff. The signs may
differ between students, and the

00:47:41.236 --> 00:47:45.800
meaning of an observation needs to be
checked rather than assumed. A shared

00:47:45.801 --> 00:47:50.427
understanding can help people respond more
consistently and earlier. It also

00:47:50.428 --> 00:47:55.054
gives us a link to our later partnership
work: making room for the student's

00:47:55.055 --> 00:47:59.435
communication, bringing different
perspectives together, and considering

00:47:59.436 --> 00:48:01.594
what support the setting can offer.

00:48:03.121 --> 00:48:07.660
That brings us to morning tea. The
workshop schedule allows a break from ten

00:48:07.661 --> 00:48:12.201
forty-five to eleven; follow the timing
arranged for your session. After the

00:48:12.202 --> 00:48:16.560
break, we'll bring sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals into

00:48:16.561 --> 00:48:21.040
communication, connection and managing
daily capacity. We'll map demand and

00:48:21.041 --> 00:48:25.701
capacity across an ordinary secondary
school day, keeping the same students in

00:48:25.702 --> 00:48:29.878
mind. Keep your notes. The strengths
you've named, the questions about

00:48:29.879 --> 00:48:34.600
routines and the things you still want to
understand will all be useful when we

00:48:34.601 --> 00:48:35.023
return.

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:48.133
Welcome back. We’re moving now from our
shared understanding of autism into the

00:48:48.133 --> 00:48:53.160
experience of a secondary school day.
You’ll find the Session Three summary on

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:57.992
page ten. Bring back the two or three
students you’ve been holding in mind,

00:48:57.992 --> 00:49:02.628
with their different strengths,
preferences and experiences. We’ll begin

00:49:02.628 --> 00:49:07.721
with communication and connection, then
map the day across the timetable. We’ll

00:49:07.721 --> 00:49:12.683
consider masking in the teenage years and
audit one part of the day that runs

00:49:12.683 --> 00:49:17.776
across classes. Those pieces will become
the starting point for Learning Design

00:49:17.776 --> 00:49:23.130
after lunch. What one teacher sees in one
period is only part of the student’s day.

00:49:23.130 --> 00:49:28.092
Rooms, adults, expectations, sensory
demands and opportunities for connection

00:49:28.092 --> 00:49:33.446
can change with every bell. The timetable
doesn’t tell us everything about how that

00:49:33.446 --> 00:49:38.669
feels or what it takes to participate. As
we work, keep examples within education

00:49:38.669 --> 00:49:43.240
practice and leave out identifying
details. Stay curious about what you

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:48.332
don’t yet know. We’re looking at how the
day is organised and experienced, with

00:49:48.332 --> 00:49:53.163
student, family and colleague knowledge
helping us see more of the picture.

00:49:54.681 --> 00:50:00.036
Let’s begin with communication. Speech is
one way of communicating, and we’ll be

00:50:00.037 --> 00:50:05.053
looking at a much wider picture: how
students receive information, how they

00:50:05.054 --> 00:50:10.545
share what matters to them, and the ways
we make both possible. The amount someone

00:50:10.546 --> 00:50:15.698
speaks doesn’t tell us how much they
understand. A student may need different

00:50:15.699 --> 00:50:21.257
ways to understand an instruction, answer
a question, ask for help, request a pause

00:50:21.258 --> 00:50:26.546
or say no. All of those messages matter.
Communication access belongs in lesson

00:50:26.547 --> 00:50:31.698
and task design in every subject, and
across the whole school day. We’ll talk

00:50:31.699 --> 00:50:35.902
about practical options, including
augmentative and alternative

00:50:35.903 --> 00:50:40.919
communication, or A A C. There isn’t one
approach that suits every student.

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:45.868
Individual assessment and intervention
questions belong with the student’s

00:50:45.869 --> 00:50:51.292
speech pathologist or relevant team; our
focus here is what educators can notice,

00:50:51.293 --> 00:50:53.053
make available and change.

00:50:54.561 --> 00:50:59.783
Let’s start with a quick, quiet think.
Since you woke up this morning, how have

00:50:59.784 --> 00:51:05.208
you communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you spoke,

00:51:05.209 --> 00:51:10.297
sent a message, pointed, showed someone a
photo, moved away, or took a moment

00:51:10.298 --> 00:51:15.186
before answering. Give yourself about
twenty seconds. Then, if you’re with

00:51:15.187 --> 00:51:20.275
colleagues, two or three people might
share one example. Sharing is optional.

00:51:20.276 --> 00:51:24.560
Pause the video now if you’d like that
thinking and sharing time.

00:51:32.561 --> 00:51:38.256
Let’s come back together. We move between
ways of communicating all day, often

00:51:38.257 --> 00:51:42.768
without noticing. Writing, facial
expression, gesture, sign, a

00:51:42.769 --> 00:51:48.685
communication device and speech are all
real communication. One method isn’t more

00:51:48.686 --> 00:51:54.455
valid because it is spoken. That gives us
something useful to notice at school.

00:51:54.456 --> 00:51:59.781
When we say, ‘Use your words,’ are we
overlooking a message a student has

00:51:59.782 --> 00:52:05.476
already communicated? A point, a look, or
moving closer or further away can be

00:52:05.477 --> 00:52:11.468
worth attending to. Our job is to listen
across the ways that person communicates,

00:52:11.469 --> 00:52:15.609
while checking what a message means rather
than assuming.

00:52:17.121 --> 00:52:21.904
There are two parts to keep distinct here:
understanding and expressing.

00:52:21.905 --> 00:52:27.161
Understanding is information coming in and
making sense. That might be a spoken

00:52:27.162 --> 00:52:32.080
instruction, a written sequence, a
demonstration, or a gesture in context.

00:52:32.081 --> 00:52:37.269
Expressing is a message going out: an
idea, a feeling, a question or a choice,

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:42.255
through any communication method. Across
secondary school, that information

00:52:42.256 --> 00:52:47.242
includes instructions from several
teachers, subject vocabulary, assessment

00:52:47.243 --> 00:52:52.296
details, timetable changes and what
happens in corridors or at breaks. These

00:52:52.297 --> 00:52:57.552
don’t necessarily look the same for one
person. Someone may speak at length and

00:52:57.553 --> 00:53:02.876
still need support to understand spoken
information. Someone else may understand

00:53:02.877 --> 00:53:08.267
much more than they can readily express.
Neither fluent speech nor limited speech

00:53:08.268 --> 00:53:13.455
gives us the whole picture. Communication
can also change with the person, the

00:53:13.456 --> 00:53:18.442
place, the demands and how someone is
feeling. A A C means augmentative and

00:53:18.443 --> 00:53:23.900
alternative communication. It can include
gesture, signs, a communication board or

00:53:23.901 --> 00:53:29.224
book, typing, or a communication device.
Some people use it alongside speech and

00:53:29.225 --> 00:53:34.076
some instead of speech. Repeated or
scripted words can carry meaning too.

00:53:34.077 --> 00:53:38.995
Listen for the context and possible
purpose before correcting the form. We

00:53:38.996 --> 00:53:42.634
don’t need to assign a language-processing
label before

00:53:42.635 --> 00:53:47.755
respecting a student’s communication. If
we need an individual assessment, we

00:53:47.756 --> 00:53:52.809
involve the relevant team. For our
classroom practice, the question is: what

00:53:52.810 --> 00:53:57.931
does this environment help the student
understand, and what ways does it give

00:53:57.932 --> 00:53:59.009
them to respond?

00:54:00.520 --> 00:54:04.760
We’re going to hear Autistic people
describe communication in their own

00:54:04.760 --> 00:54:09.364
words. As you watch, notice what supports
communication. Choose one idea that

00:54:09.364 --> 00:54:13.907
stands out to you. Let the speakers’
experiences lead your thinking, without

00:54:13.907 --> 00:54:17.238
expecting one account to describe every
Autistic person.

00:59:49.720 --> 00:59:54.220
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? Take a moment to

00:59:54.221 --> 00:59:59.214
choose one idea. You might think about a
communication strength, something another

00:59:59.215 --> 01:00:03.468
person did that helped, or the difference
the environment or available

01:00:03.469 --> 01:00:08.092
communication method made. If you’re
watching with colleagues, invite one or

01:00:08.093 --> 01:00:11.360
two brief responses. Pause here for that
conversation.

01:00:19.361 --> 01:00:23.815
As we come back, hold onto that specific
idea. There is no single Autistic

01:00:23.816 --> 01:00:28.148
communication profile, so we need to
listen to what helps the particular

01:00:28.149 --> 01:00:32.786
person. One practical point to carry into
school is availability. A student’s

01:00:32.787 --> 01:00:37.424
communication system needs to be where
communication happens: in every period

01:00:37.425 --> 01:00:42.122
and with every teacher, at break, during
transitions and when plans change. It

01:00:42.123 --> 01:00:46.944
cannot be limited to one activity or a
therapy session. We support the student’s

01:00:46.945 --> 01:00:51.642
ways of communicating, working with the
student, their family and the relevant

01:00:51.643 --> 01:00:55.303
team. We aren’t prescribing a system from
a workshop example.

01:00:56.841 --> 01:01:01.993
Turn to page eleven of your Participant
Guide, or use a sheet of paper. Put one

01:01:01.994 --> 01:01:07.147
of the students you have in mind in the
centre. Around them are home, teachers,

01:01:07.148 --> 01:01:12.432
friends, community and support staff. Who
is part of this student’s communication

01:01:12.433 --> 01:01:17.189
network? Add just one person or place now.
Use roles rather than names: a

01:01:17.190 --> 01:01:22.409
family member, a classmate, a subject or
relief teacher, someone at a club, or a

01:01:22.410 --> 01:01:27.430
member of learning support or wellbeing
staff. You can write, draw or use dot

01:01:27.431 --> 01:01:32.716
points. If it helps, ask a colleague to
scribe your ideas. You don’t need to fill

01:01:32.717 --> 01:01:37.803
every space. Networks can be small or
changing, and an empty box is fine. Give

01:01:37.804 --> 01:01:42.824
yourself about a minute and a half. Pause
the video while you make that first

01:01:42.825 --> 01:01:43.419
addition.

01:01:51.441 --> 01:01:56.759
Keep that map open. You can return to the
rest later. Different people and places

01:01:56.760 --> 01:02:02.078
may reveal different parts of a student’s
communication. A family member, subject

01:02:02.079 --> 01:02:07.065
teacher, year level coordinator and
support worker may each notice something

01:02:07.066 --> 01:02:12.517
valuable. Friends in person or online may
also be an important part of a teenager’s

01:02:12.518 --> 01:02:17.836
communication network. We’re not deciding
who understands the student best. We’re

01:02:17.837 --> 01:02:23.023
recognising that the picture is shared.
Where we know little about part of that

01:02:23.024 --> 01:02:28.209
network, we have a question to explore
with the student and family, rather than

01:02:28.210 --> 01:02:30.402
a gap to fill with an assumption.

01:02:31.921 --> 01:02:37.086
Now let’s think about what should travel
between those people and places, so the

01:02:37.087 --> 01:02:41.859
student doesn’t have to start again with
every new person. We’ll use three

01:02:41.860 --> 01:02:46.566
colours. Purple is a communication
strength. Blue is something that helps

01:02:46.567 --> 01:02:51.405
communication. Orange is something you
would like others to know. Write one

01:02:51.406 --> 01:02:56.440
note, two, or all three. You can use one
of your students, without identifying

01:02:56.441 --> 01:03:01.409
them, or a made-up example. If you don’t
have coloured notes, label the three

01:03:01.410 --> 01:03:06.771
categories on paper. This is also on page
eleven. Allow about two minutes to write.

01:03:06.772 --> 01:03:11.675
Then, if you’re together, spend a few
minutes sharing. You can add a note to

01:03:11.676 --> 01:03:16.709
the board or read one out, and you can
keep your notes private. Leave any note

01:03:16.710 --> 01:03:21.613
with identifying details off the shared
board. Pause now for the writing and

01:03:21.614 --> 01:03:22.725
optional sharing.

01:03:30.761 --> 01:03:35.023
Let’s bring our attention back. Look
across the ideas available to you. What

01:03:35.024 --> 01:03:39.002
similarities do you notice? What strengths
might be missed if we looked

01:03:39.003 --> 01:03:43.549
only for speech? Which supports involve
changing what other people do or provide?

01:03:43.550 --> 01:03:47.811
Think about what would help a relief
teacher, a new subject teacher or staff

01:03:47.812 --> 01:03:51.904
on duty at recess and lunch. Useful
knowledge can travel across subjects,

01:03:51.905 --> 01:03:55.826
rooms, teachers, relief days and home,
with the student’s and family’s

01:03:55.827 --> 01:03:59.747
perspectives included. It can make
continuity possible when the people

01:03:59.748 --> 01:04:03.897
around a student change. Keep the blue
notes in view: the things that help

01:04:03.898 --> 01:04:08.329
communication. We’re going to connect
those ideas with three practical options.

01:04:09.841 --> 01:04:15.225
The first option is clear communication.
Use familiar, specific words and give one

01:04:15.226 --> 01:04:20.343
idea or step at a time, in a form and
amount the person can access. That might

01:04:20.344 --> 01:04:25.529
mean making a direction shorter or making
the first step clearer. The second is

01:04:25.530 --> 01:04:29.783
processing time. After giving information,
pause. A person may be

01:04:29.784 --> 01:04:35.035
making sense of what you said, organising
a response and communicating it. If we

01:04:35.036 --> 01:04:40.287
fill every pause with a new version of the
question, we may add more information

01:04:40.288 --> 01:04:45.472
before the earlier information has been
processed. The third is visual support:

01:04:45.473 --> 01:04:50.857
showing information as well as saying it,
so there is something to return to. With

01:04:50.858 --> 01:04:55.377
several teachers in a day, useful guidance
needs to be shared so each

01:04:55.378 --> 01:05:00.230
teacher knows what helps. These are
options to match to the person and the

01:05:00.231 --> 01:05:05.282
moment, rather than a checklist that
guarantees success. Keep the student’s A

01:05:05.283 --> 01:05:10.601
A C available and accept their preferred
ways of responding, including refusal. A

01:05:10.602 --> 01:05:15.520
useful communication support makes
messages easier to understand and share.

01:05:15.521 --> 01:05:20.971
It doesn’t require the student to produce
the answer or communication style we were

01:05:20.972 --> 01:05:21.636
expecting.

01:05:23.161 --> 01:05:28.392
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to. The three examples

01:05:28.393 --> 01:05:33.227
here answer different questions. A timer
can show how much time is left. A

01:05:33.228 --> 01:05:38.326
sequence can show what to do next. A daily
schedule can show what is happening

01:05:38.327 --> 01:05:43.691
today. You may already use a calendar, a
shopping list or a reminder on your phone

01:05:43.692 --> 01:05:48.658
in much the same way. The format needs to
make sense to the person. Objects,

01:05:48.659 --> 01:05:53.559
photographs, written words and symbols can
all be options, on paper or on a

01:05:53.560 --> 01:05:57.665
device. Check that the student understands
what the information

01:05:57.666 --> 01:06:02.698
represents and can reach it when it is
needed. A support displayed across the

01:06:02.699 --> 01:06:07.599
room may exist without being useful at the
moment of need. For adolescents,

01:06:07.600 --> 01:06:12.831
formats such as a phone calendar, written
task list or checklist in the learning

01:06:12.832 --> 01:06:17.732
platform may feel more comfortable because
other students use them too. Ask

01:06:17.733 --> 01:06:22.500
about the format and whether it feels
useful and respectful. Think of the

01:06:22.501 --> 01:06:27.600
visual information already available in
your classes: lesson steps, assessment

01:06:27.601 --> 01:06:32.501
instructions, the timetable and room
changes. What question does it answer?

01:06:32.502 --> 01:06:37.534
Could a student return to it without
asking an adult? A visual support offers

01:06:37.535 --> 01:06:42.633
information; it isn’t a condition the
student must satisfy to access something

01:06:42.634 --> 01:06:48.064
they enjoy. Visual supports and A A C can
overlap, but they aren’t interchangeable.

01:06:48.065 --> 01:06:53.230
Keep the person’s communication methods
available as well. We’ll return to this

01:06:53.231 --> 01:06:58.462
in Session Four when you choose a design
option for a lesson, assessment task or

01:06:58.463 --> 01:06:59.058
handover.

01:07:00.561 --> 01:07:05.560
Double empathy asks us to look at
understanding in both directions. When

01:07:05.561 --> 01:07:10.912
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, it can be harder

01:07:10.913 --> 01:07:15.489
for them to understand one another. That
doesn’t mean every social

01:07:15.490 --> 01:07:20.982
misunderstanding sits inside the Autistic
student. Consider a class discussion,

01:07:20.983 --> 01:07:26.193
group task, corridor interaction or online
conversation. Whose expectations

01:07:26.194 --> 01:07:31.474
are shaping what counts as joining in? Who
is expected to adjust? What would

01:07:31.475 --> 01:07:36.967
make this interaction clearer or safer for
the people involved? For example, an

01:07:36.968 --> 01:07:41.756
adult might expect a quick spoken response
as a sign of connection. A

01:07:41.757 --> 01:07:47.248
student might connect through a shared
activity, through detailed talk about an

01:07:47.249 --> 01:07:52.530
interest, or with more time between turns.
Those are possible differences to

01:07:52.531 --> 01:07:58.023
explore, not conclusions to impose on a
particular student. The question is how

01:07:58.024 --> 01:08:03.586
each student connects. Connection doesn’t
need to look one way. Our expectations

01:08:03.587 --> 01:08:08.656
and the context shape the interaction too,
so we have something useful to

01:08:08.657 --> 01:08:14.149
examine and change. We can make room for
different ways of being together while

01:08:14.150 --> 01:08:18.020
continuing to listen to what each person
actually wants.

01:08:19.561 --> 01:08:25.125
Let’s carry that idea into the many ways
students connect. As we watch this next

01:08:25.126 --> 01:08:30.339
clip, keep the question open: how does
each student connect? Notice people,

01:08:30.340 --> 01:08:35.763
places, activities and ways of joining. A
different style of connection is not

01:08:35.764 --> 01:08:38.228
evidence that connection is absent.

01:09:23.321 --> 01:09:27.536
Keep those possibilities in mind as we
widen the picture beyond a single

01:09:27.537 --> 01:09:32.167
interaction. We can look for connection in
a student’s preferred activities and

01:09:32.168 --> 01:09:36.322
relationships, while allowing their own
ways of showing it to guide us.

01:09:37.841 --> 01:09:42.427
A student’s social world can include
family, school, community and online

01:09:42.428 --> 01:09:47.078
spaces. Connection might be one to one,
working alongside someone, gaming,

01:09:47.079 --> 01:09:51.856
sharing a great deal about an interest, or
seeing someone only occasionally.

01:09:51.857 --> 01:09:56.889
Being together quietly can matter as much
as a lively conversation. Culture also

01:09:56.890 --> 01:10:01.795
shapes expectations about interaction and
relationships. We need to be careful

01:10:01.796 --> 01:10:06.509
about treating the pattern most familiar
to us as the measure of meaningful

01:10:06.510 --> 01:10:11.287
connection for everyone. There may be
spaces outside home and school where a

01:10:11.288 --> 01:10:16.065
student feels a particular sense of
belonging: a club, a community activity,

01:10:16.066 --> 01:10:20.971
or another shared-interest space. For many
adolescents, online connections and

01:10:20.972 --> 01:10:26.004
shared-interest groups are valued parts of
their social lives too. We don’t need

01:10:26.005 --> 01:10:30.782
to disclose a student’s online activity,
identity or community membership to

01:10:30.783 --> 01:10:35.879
recognise that these possibilities exist.
Keep the picture individual. What do we

01:10:35.880 --> 01:10:40.657
know about this student’s preferred
people, places and ways of joining? What

01:10:40.658 --> 01:10:43.970
are we assuming? What could we find out
respectfully?

01:10:45.481 --> 01:10:50.612
Find a partner and turn to the Connection
discussion on page twelve. Where do the

01:10:50.613 --> 01:10:55.487
students you have in mind spend recess and
lunch, and who with? What kinds of

01:10:55.488 --> 01:11:00.491
connection does each enjoy? Think about
shared interests, gaming, sport, music,

01:11:00.492 --> 01:11:05.238
clubs, online communities or being
together quietly. Which parts of the day

01:11:05.239 --> 01:11:10.178
are socially hardest for them: group work,
corridors, the canteen or P E? What

01:11:10.179 --> 01:11:15.245
could teachers change to make connection
easier without putting a student on the

01:11:15.246 --> 01:11:19.928
spot? If you don’t know where a student
goes during breaks, keep that as a

01:11:19.929 --> 01:11:24.419
question to follow up. Spending time alone
or in a quiet place may be a

01:11:24.420 --> 01:11:29.615
preference, so avoid treating difference
as proof that connection is missing. Keep

01:11:29.616 --> 01:11:34.298
online activity, identity and community
membership private. Use about five

01:11:34.299 --> 01:11:39.302
minutes. You can reflect individually if
you’re watching on your own. Pause the

01:11:39.303 --> 01:11:41.160
video now for the discussion.

01:11:49.201 --> 01:11:53.748
Let’s come back to the last question: what
could educators change around the

01:11:53.749 --> 01:11:58.417
student? If you’re together, you might
offer one practical possibility without

01:11:58.418 --> 01:12:02.904
describing the student. Notice whether
your idea makes room for the kind of

01:12:02.905 --> 01:12:07.451
connection the student values. Does it
change an expectation, an activity, a

01:12:07.452 --> 01:12:12.363
space or the pace of interaction? Does it
leave room for choosing when to join and

01:12:12.364 --> 01:12:17.153
when to step back? Could you make group
roles clearer in advance, offer a choice

01:12:17.154 --> 01:12:21.883
of partners, or make a quiet space or club
available without singling a student

01:12:21.884 --> 01:12:26.430
out? The aim is meaningful connection for
that person. We don’t need to make

01:12:26.431 --> 01:12:28.672
everyone look social in the same way.

01:12:30.201 --> 01:12:35.838
Now we’ll look at daily capacity.
Communication and connection are part of

01:12:35.839 --> 01:12:41.090
a much larger day, and that day asks
different amounts of energy from

01:12:41.091 --> 01:12:46.806
different students. As we move into the
next activities, we’ll use a simple

01:12:46.807 --> 01:12:52.985
question: what costs, and what lightens?
That language will help us look beyond a

01:12:52.986 --> 01:12:59.009
single task to the pattern of demands and
opportunities for recovery across the

01:12:59.010 --> 01:12:59.317
day.

01:13:00.841 --> 01:13:05.637
Think of a student’s available energy as a
battery. Each day starts with some

01:13:05.638 --> 01:13:10.434
charge, and it isn’t necessarily the same
amount as yesterday. Activities and

01:13:10.435 --> 01:13:14.978
demands draw on that energy. As the
battery runs lower, managing the next

01:13:14.979 --> 01:13:19.712
demand can become harder. You may also
hear people in the Autistic community

01:13:19.713 --> 01:13:24.698
talk about spoons. The idea is having a
limited supply available: an action uses

01:13:24.699 --> 01:13:29.559
one spoon or several, and when spoons are
low, there is less left for the next

01:13:29.560 --> 01:13:34.355
thing. These are ways of talking about
capacity, rather than a measure we can

01:13:34.356 --> 01:13:39.089
calculate for someone else. The other part
of the picture is recharging. The

01:13:39.090 --> 01:13:44.076
right supports can reduce demand and help
restore some capacity. A pause, useful

01:13:44.077 --> 01:13:48.620
information, a preferred way to
communicate or a safe connection may make

01:13:48.621 --> 01:13:53.543
a difference, depending on the person. So
we’ll look for both sides in a school

01:13:53.544 --> 01:13:58.088
day: what draws on capacity, and what
helps to restore it. What helps one

01:13:58.089 --> 01:14:00.421
student may be different for another.

01:14:01.961 --> 01:14:07.275
Capacity fluctuates. Period six may look
very different from period one. Like all

01:14:07.276 --> 01:14:12.391
of us, Autistic students can have easier
days and harder days. Stress, energy,

01:14:12.392 --> 01:14:16.909
sensory demands and what else is happening
in life can change what is

01:14:16.910 --> 01:14:22.024
manageable. That means the same student
may do something one day and need more

01:14:22.025 --> 01:14:26.741
support with it the next. Yesterday’s
success doesn’t prove that today’s

01:14:26.742 --> 01:14:31.923
difficulty is a choice, and finishing a
task doesn’t tell us how much effort it

01:14:31.924 --> 01:14:37.371
required. Hold that in mind when you look
at the students you know. We can ask what

01:14:37.372 --> 01:14:42.487
is different today, what demands have
already accumulated, and what support is

01:14:42.488 --> 01:14:47.337
available now. We don’t have to know every
cause before making information

01:14:47.338 --> 01:14:52.586
clearer or allowing a useful pause. We do
need to keep checking with the student

01:14:52.587 --> 01:14:57.037
and the people who know them, instead of
treating capacity as fixed.

01:14:58.561 --> 01:15:03.659
Six classrooms, six teachers, one student.
Think about one student’s day

01:15:03.660 --> 01:15:09.116
from period one to period six. How many
rooms, adults and corridor trips does

01:15:09.117 --> 01:15:14.718
that involve? Take a moment to count the
changes, rather than only the lessons.

01:15:14.719 --> 01:15:20.391
Each change can involve leaving a task,
moving through a busy space and arriving

01:15:20.392 --> 01:15:25.202
at a different subject, set of
expectations and sensory environment.

01:15:25.203 --> 01:15:30.875
Each teacher sees one part of that day,
often with little information about what

01:15:30.876 --> 01:15:36.333
came before. Something that helps in
period one may be unknown in period four

01:15:36.334 --> 01:15:41.647
unless there is a way to carry that
knowledge across the timetable. Sharing

01:15:41.648 --> 01:15:47.464
what works is something a school can plan
for. We’ll return to it in Session Four,

01:15:47.465 --> 01:15:52.921
including a short profile written with the
student and shared with the people

01:15:52.922 --> 01:15:58.738
teaching them. For now, count the rooms,
teachers and transitions in that one day.

01:15:58.739 --> 01:16:04.627
You can make a brief note beside your day
map. Pause if you need longer to think it

01:16:04.628 --> 01:16:05.200
through.

01:16:13.241 --> 01:16:18.698
Keep those changes in view. The point is
to put together what one period alone

01:16:18.699 --> 01:16:24.227
can’t show. Information shared clearly can
save each teacher having to work out

01:16:24.228 --> 01:16:29.968
the same support again, and can help the
student experience more continuity. Let’s

01:16:29.969 --> 01:16:32.093
put the whole day on one page.

01:16:33.601 --> 01:16:39.396
Open page thirteen, Map a secondary school
day. Use a real timetable for one

01:16:39.397 --> 01:16:44.806
of your students, with identifying details
removed, or use the supplied

01:16:44.807 --> 01:16:50.525
sample Year Nine timetable. Go from
arrival to departure, period by period.

01:16:50.526 --> 01:16:56.243
Note the room, the teacher and what
happens. Include corridors, lockers and

01:16:56.244 --> 01:17:01.808
breaks, changes or relief teachers, and
opportunities to choose, pause or

01:17:01.809 --> 01:17:07.913
recover. Follow the whole day, even if you
only teach one period of it. For each

01:17:07.914 --> 01:17:14.018
of the students you have in mind, mark C
if a part of the day costs energy, L if

01:17:14.019 --> 01:17:20.123
it lightens or gives energy back, and a
question mark if you are not sure yet. A

01:17:20.124 --> 01:17:26.229
period can carry both C and L. These are
prompts for inquiry rather than a score

01:17:26.230 --> 01:17:31.948
or a claim about what someone feels. The
sample timetable includes a relief

01:17:31.949 --> 01:17:37.821
teacher in Science, a quiz announced at
the start of Maths, changing for P E,

01:17:37.822 --> 01:17:43.540
group work and an assessment task handed
out in the final period. Those are

01:17:43.541 --> 01:17:49.027
fictional planning details to examine, not
a prediction of any student’s

01:17:49.028 --> 01:17:55.132
response. Take about seven minutes, with
optional discussion in a pair. Keep the

01:17:55.133 --> 01:18:01.237
map private unless you choose to discuss
it, and leave room to add detail on the

01:18:01.238 --> 01:18:04.481
next slide. Pause the video while you
work.

01:18:12.521 --> 01:18:17.427
Let’s pause our drawing there. You don’t
need a polished or complete map. This is

01:18:17.428 --> 01:18:22.150
a working picture to extend, rather than a
judgement of a student or a school.

01:18:22.151 --> 01:18:26.812
Look briefly at the same point in the day
for your different students. It may

01:18:26.813 --> 01:18:31.781
involve different communication, sensory
or task demands for each of them. That is

01:18:31.782 --> 01:18:36.688
why we’re keeping several real students in
view. Keep the map beside you; it will

01:18:36.689 --> 01:18:38.711
travel with us into Session Four.

01:18:40.240 --> 01:18:45.979
Now turn to page fourteen and return to
your day map. Use one mark or colour for

01:18:45.980 --> 01:18:50.629
what costs: what adds demand or
uncertainty. Use another for what

01:18:50.630 --> 01:18:56.442
lightens: what reduces demand, restores
capacity or brings joy. Think back to the

01:18:56.443 --> 01:19:01.746
battery image: what drains, and what might
recharge? Add questions where a

01:19:01.747 --> 01:19:06.251
student’s experience isn’t known.
Something can be enjoyable or

01:19:06.252 --> 01:19:11.773
successfully completed and still take
considerable effort. We can record what

01:19:11.774 --> 01:19:17.296
we observe, but we can’t read an internal
state from a single moment. Include

01:19:17.297 --> 01:19:22.455
strengths, joy, safe connection and
belonging. We want a picture of what

01:19:22.456 --> 01:19:27.686
sustains a student as well as what is
difficult. Give yourself about four

01:19:27.687 --> 01:19:31.973
minutes to add this layer, and pause the
video while you do.

01:19:40.001 --> 01:19:45.105
Come back to the pattern across the day.
Where do several demands occur close

01:19:45.106 --> 01:19:50.210
together? Where are the opportunities for
relief or recovery? What is already

01:19:50.211 --> 01:19:55.650
working well and worth protecting? Treat
these marks as prompts for inquiry, not a

01:19:55.651 --> 01:20:00.621
score. What costs or lightens can differ
between students, and for the same

01:20:00.622 --> 01:20:05.524
student on different days. Choose one
pattern you would like to understand

01:20:05.525 --> 01:20:10.764
better. That will help when we audit a
part of the day that runs across classes

01:20:10.765 --> 01:20:11.300
shortly.

01:20:12.801 --> 01:20:17.709
This school-day stack gives us another way
to see accumulation. The darker bars

01:20:17.710 --> 01:20:22.366
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. They’re a

01:20:22.367 --> 01:20:27.338
visual cue, rather than quantities we can
assign to a student. No single teacher

01:20:27.339 --> 01:20:32.183
sees this whole stack. Each sees a part of
it, so a moment that looks small by

01:20:32.184 --> 01:20:36.778
itself may arrive after many other
demands. The last transition of the day

01:20:36.779 --> 01:20:41.749
sits within everything that came before
it. Equally, a supportive interaction or

01:20:41.750 --> 01:20:46.280
an opportunity to recover belongs in the
picture too. So when we notice a

01:20:46.281 --> 01:20:51.189
difficult moment, we can ask what the day
has already asked of this student and

01:20:51.190 --> 01:20:55.720
what support has been available. Keep this
qualitative. We’re looking for

01:20:55.721 --> 01:21:00.252
patterns and questions that help us
improve access, while keeping relief,

01:21:00.253 --> 01:21:02.769
recovery and joy visible alongside cost.

01:21:04.281 --> 01:21:08.770
There are other things that can draw on
our batteries or spoons, including effort

01:21:08.771 --> 01:21:13.204
that isn’t obvious to someone watching.
Let’s hear the next video about masking.

01:21:13.205 --> 01:21:17.637
Keep in mind the difference between how a
person appears on the outside and what

01:21:17.638 --> 01:21:18.815
they may be managing.

01:22:49.241 --> 01:22:54.828
As we return from the video, keep that
distinction open. Visible behaviour gives

01:22:54.829 --> 01:22:59.992
us part of the picture. We’ll now connect
this with identity, capacity and

01:22:59.993 --> 01:23:01.407
belonging at school.

01:23:02.921 --> 01:23:08.151
A student can appear fine without feeling
fine. Quietness, compliance or completed

01:23:08.152 --> 01:23:13.189
work doesn’t give us a full account of
what someone is managing. Masking brings

01:23:13.190 --> 01:23:17.709
that question into our thinking about
identity and belonging. Hold four

01:23:17.710 --> 01:23:22.230
questions in mind. What might this student
be working hard to manage or

01:23:22.231 --> 01:23:27.332
hide? Where can they be recognised and
included? What supports belonging without

01:23:27.333 --> 01:23:32.434
requiring them to appear less Autistic?
And what does the student want others to

01:23:32.435 --> 01:23:36.955
understand? We can’t answer those
questions simply by watching from the

01:23:36.956 --> 01:23:41.669
outside. They invite us to listen and to
examine the conditions around the

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:46.513
student. Include what feels safe and
joyful, the connections they value, and

01:23:46.514 --> 01:23:51.809
places where they can be themselves. This
is a reason to look beyond whether a task

01:23:51.810 --> 01:23:56.394
was finished or a lesson was quiet. Access
matters, and so do the effort

01:23:56.395 --> 01:23:59.622
involved and the student’s experience of
belonging.

01:24:01.161 --> 01:24:06.070
The teenage years can bring a strong
pressure to fit in. As peer acceptance

01:24:06.071 --> 01:24:10.714
becomes more important, a young person may
work harder to hide parts of

01:24:10.715 --> 01:24:15.956
themselves. Working out who you are while
also managing how you appear to others

01:24:15.957 --> 01:24:20.932
takes energy. This connects masking with
the capacity we’ve been discussing.

01:24:20.933 --> 01:24:26.107
Burnout and what the slide calls School
Can’t can build over time. School Can’t

01:24:26.108 --> 01:24:31.017
describes a student being unable to
attend, and needs to be approached with

01:24:31.018 --> 01:24:35.926
care rather than treated as defiance.
We’ll return to working with families

01:24:35.927 --> 01:24:41.035
around capacity in Session Five. The
picture also includes growth. When school

01:24:41.036 --> 01:24:45.945
feels safe, young people can build
self-understanding, recognise what helps

01:24:45.946 --> 01:24:50.921
them and practise asking for what they
need. We can support that through the

01:24:50.922 --> 01:24:55.632
environments, relationships and
communication choices we make available.

01:24:55.633 --> 01:25:00.608
None of this tells us what every teenager
experiences. Keep listening to the

01:25:00.609 --> 01:25:05.916
individual young person, including where
they feel most able to be themselves and

01:25:05.917 --> 01:25:10.096
what they want other people to understand.
This may connect with

01:25:10.097 --> 01:25:14.939
personal experience for some people
watching. You are welcome to step out,

01:25:14.940 --> 01:25:19.915
pause and return. There is no need to
share a personal or identifying story.

01:25:19.916 --> 01:25:25.356
Carry forward the last line on the slide:
feeling safe to be themselves helps young

01:25:25.357 --> 01:25:27.677
people speak up for what they need.

01:25:30.681 --> 01:25:35.661
The phrase on this slide is, ‘Good and
chilled in class.’ A quiet student who

01:25:35.662 --> 01:25:40.970
causes no disruption may still be working
very hard. Calm doesn’t always mean that

01:25:40.971 --> 01:25:46.147
everything is okay; sometimes effort or
difficulty is hidden. Hold this question

01:25:46.148 --> 01:25:51.259
privately: who is your quietest Autistic
student, and what do you actually know

01:25:51.260 --> 01:25:56.306
about their experience? What have they
communicated, and what are you assuming

01:25:56.307 --> 01:26:01.483
from how they look in your lesson? You
don’t need to answer aloud. Give yourself

01:26:01.484 --> 01:26:06.726
a moment to notice what is known and what
you would like to understand. We’ll use

01:26:06.727 --> 01:26:11.379
the next discussion to consider how useful
information reaches the right

01:26:11.380 --> 01:26:14.982
people. Pause here if you need longer with
the question.

01:26:23.001 --> 01:26:27.717
Return to page twelve for the masking
discussion. With a partner, consider the

01:26:27.718 --> 01:26:32.372
students you have in mind across the whole
timetable. Which classes, teachers

01:26:32.373 --> 01:26:37.272
or times of day ask the most of them to
appear fine? Where at school do they seem

01:26:37.273 --> 01:26:41.927
most able to be themselves? Who would
notice if they were struggling, and how

01:26:41.928 --> 01:26:46.521
would that information reach the right
people? Use about three minutes. Keep

01:26:46.522 --> 01:26:50.809
identifying details private and
distinguish what you know from what you

01:26:50.810 --> 01:26:55.526
wonder. The last question is about the way
the school shares information among

01:26:55.527 --> 01:26:59.936
subject teachers, year level staff,
learning support, wellbeing and home.

01:26:59.937 --> 01:27:04.285
Keep the focus on that process rather than
naming a colleague who missed

01:27:04.286 --> 01:27:07.163
something. Pause the video for the
conversation.

01:27:15.201 --> 01:27:19.797
Let’s come back to how the information
would travel. Who would notice, who needs

01:27:19.798 --> 01:27:24.510
to know, and what process connects them?
If there is a gap, keep it as a practical

01:27:24.511 --> 01:27:28.991
question to follow up. A possible change
might involve asking the student more

01:27:28.992 --> 01:27:33.471
accessibly, sharing guidance between
teachers, or protecting an existing place

01:27:33.472 --> 01:27:37.952
of comfort and connection. Keep the young
person’s perspective central. We are

01:27:37.953 --> 01:27:42.142
trying to make support and being
themselves more possible across the day,

01:27:42.143 --> 01:27:44.410
then find out whether the change helps.

01:27:45.921 --> 01:27:51.380
On page fifteen, choose one part of the
secondary day that runs across classes.

01:27:51.381 --> 01:27:56.769
You could examine how instructions are
given, assessment notice, corridors and

01:27:56.770 --> 01:28:02.089
lockers, relief-teacher arrangements, or
where a student can regulate between

01:28:02.090 --> 01:28:07.619
periods. Use one of those options or a
relevant focus from your map. First, name

01:28:07.620 --> 01:28:13.288
the setting or process neutrally. Second,
write what educators can observe without

01:28:13.289 --> 01:28:18.468
interpretation. Third, list possible
contributors in the environment, task,

01:28:18.469 --> 01:28:23.648
timing, communication or expectations.
Fourth, record what already lightens

01:28:23.649 --> 01:28:29.107
demand and one question to investigate.
For assessment notice, for example, you

01:28:29.108 --> 01:28:34.567
could examine when information becomes
available, how the task is explained and

01:28:34.568 --> 01:28:39.957
what remains visible afterwards. For a
handover to a relief teacher, you could

01:28:39.958 --> 01:28:44.786
examine what guidance is available before
the lesson begins. These are

01:28:44.787 --> 01:28:49.966
possibilities to explore; choose the
actual process you want to understand.

01:28:49.967 --> 01:28:55.216
Work on your own, or with a faculty or
year level colleague if you share the

01:28:55.217 --> 01:29:00.535
process. Allow about six minutes. Keep all
examples anonymised. This examines

01:29:00.536 --> 01:29:05.785
how the day is set up, rather than judging
one teacher or student. Pause the

01:29:05.786 --> 01:29:08.025
video now to complete the audit.

01:29:16.041 --> 01:29:20.306
Let’s bring that audit to a close. Check
your wording once more. Have you

01:29:20.307 --> 01:29:25.164
separated what you observed from what you
think might explain it? Have you included

01:29:25.165 --> 01:29:29.666
what already helps? Select one focus to
carry into the next session. A useful

01:29:29.667 --> 01:29:34.110
starting point is specific enough to
investigate and within the influence of

01:29:34.111 --> 01:29:38.612
educators or the school. Keep both the day
map and this audit. Together, they

01:29:38.613 --> 01:29:43.113
will help you describe an access question
and make a practical design change.

01:29:44.641 --> 01:29:50.098
On page sixteen, choose a clear focus to
carry into design. There are three parts:

01:29:50.099 --> 01:29:55.488
Keep, Reduce and Explore. For Keep, name
one practice worth protecting because it

01:29:55.489 --> 01:30:00.743
supports access, connection or joy. For
Reduce, name one barrier or unnecessary

01:30:00.744 --> 01:30:05.797
demand. Tick which kind of routine that
Reduce item belongs to: a lesson, an

01:30:05.798 --> 01:30:11.187
assessment task or a handover. We’ll use
those three categories to form groups in

01:30:11.188 --> 01:30:16.375
Session Four. For Explore, write one
question to take to students, families or

01:30:16.376 --> 01:30:21.429
the team. An unanswered question is
useful; you don’t need to turn it into a

01:30:21.430 --> 01:30:26.415
conclusion today. Allow about two minutes.
Your responses can stay private.

01:30:26.416 --> 01:30:31.265
Pause the video to finish the card, then
keep it with your map and audit.

01:30:39.280 --> 01:30:44.036
Keep students first as you look at those
three choices. Design should protect what

01:30:44.037 --> 01:30:48.499
supports access, connection and joy as
well as reduce a barrier. Consultation

01:30:48.500 --> 01:30:53.021
can help us understand the questions still
open. We’ll take the map, the audit

01:30:53.022 --> 01:30:57.542
and your Reduce item into the learning
design session after lunch. In the live

01:30:57.543 --> 01:31:02.182
workshop, lunch runs from twelve thirty to
one. For this recording, you can take

01:31:02.183 --> 01:31:06.469
a break between sessions and return when
you’re ready. Before we close the

01:31:06.470 --> 01:31:10.755
morning, let’s come back to the fuller
picture of the students you’ve been

01:31:10.756 --> 01:31:11.695
holding in mind.

01:31:13.201 --> 01:31:17.929
Across Sessions Two and Three, you’ve been
building a picture of those

01:31:17.930 --> 01:31:23.205
students. How the world arrives through
their senses. How each thinks, focuses

01:31:23.206 --> 01:31:28.825
and builds understanding. Their strengths
and interests. Their internal experience.

01:31:28.826 --> 01:31:34.170
How they reach towards other people, and
what that can cost when the conditions

01:31:34.171 --> 01:31:39.379
don’t support them. Managing a school day
can involve sensory demands, social

01:31:39.380 --> 01:31:44.107
interpretation, executive effort and
communication across differences.

01:31:44.108 --> 01:31:49.110
Sometimes it also involves working to
appear fine when fine is not what is

01:31:49.111 --> 01:31:54.593
happening. That effort uses resources. A
young person who knows who they are, and

01:31:54.594 --> 01:31:59.458
feels valued for it, has more of those
resources available for learning,

01:31:59.459 --> 01:32:04.187
connection and self-knowledge. The
research discussed in this workshop

01:32:04.188 --> 01:32:09.327
connects a positive sense of Autistic
identity with better mental health and

01:32:09.328 --> 01:32:14.466
wellbeing. Belonging matters. So does
agency: meaningful choice about how to

01:32:14.467 --> 01:32:19.606
communicate, where to be, and when to step
back. Knowing that other Autistic

01:32:19.607 --> 01:32:24.609
people share your experience can matter
too. There are Autistic adults and

01:32:24.610 --> 01:32:29.612
communities in which people find belonging
and purpose. These students are

01:32:29.613 --> 01:32:34.546
not alone. This belongs within our
educational work. We can make room for

01:32:34.547 --> 01:32:38.863
identity, connection and
self-understanding in the conditions we

01:32:38.864 --> 01:32:44.414
create every day. Take that thought with
you into the break. The deepest thing you

01:32:44.415 --> 01:32:49.280
can do is support them to know themselves,
and to know that who they are

01:32:49.281 --> 01:32:50.445
is worth knowing.

01:33:00.480 --> 01:33:03.061
Welcome back. This is session four
learning design.

01:33:03.061 --> 01:33:07.358
You'll find the session summary on page
seventeen of your participant

01:33:07.358 --> 01:33:07.868
guide.

01:33:07.868 --> 01:33:12.415
Have your secondary day map, day audit and
Carry into design

01:33:12.415 --> 01:33:14.236
card from session three beside you.

01:33:14.236 --> 01:33:18.242
We're going to use what you noticed to
make one small testable

01:33:18.242 --> 01:33:22.498
change to a lesson, an assessment task or
a handover.

01:33:22.498 --> 01:33:25.245
The direction matters.

01:33:25.557 --> 01:33:28.731
We begin with the students and their
experience, then consider

01:33:28.731 --> 01:33:31.738
what can change in the environment, the
task or the routine.

01:33:31.738 --> 01:33:34.631
What we know about a student is useful
knowledge for design.

01:33:34.631 --> 01:33:38.460
It helps us understand each student in
context.

01:33:38.460 --> 01:33:42.757
Keep your reduce item in view, the barrier
or unnecessary demand

01:33:42.757 --> 01:33:44.391
you chose to explore.

01:33:44.391 --> 01:33:47.981
We'll work with others to design a
response, then each of you

01:33:47.981 --> 01:33:50.946
will check it against the two or three
students you have in mind.

01:33:50.946 --> 01:33:55.254
The aim is something practical enough to
try, observe and refine.

01:33:58.000 --> 01:34:03.107
Students come first. Before choosing a
strategy, return to the real students and

01:34:03.108 --> 01:34:07.762
the evidence on your day map. What matters
to them? Which costs from your

01:34:07.763 --> 01:34:12.870
day map does the design need to address,
and which strengths or interests can it

01:34:12.871 --> 01:34:17.655
build on? What is the specific access
question? What have you observed, and

01:34:17.656 --> 01:34:22.310
what are you still wondering? We’ll design
one lesson, assessment task or

01:34:22.311 --> 01:34:27.353
handover, then test that design against
different students. Keep an observation

01:34:27.354 --> 01:34:32.332
separate from an interpretation, and a
question separate from a conclusion. If

01:34:32.333 --> 01:34:37.569
you don’t know how a student experiences
something, leave room to find out through

01:34:37.570 --> 01:34:42.354
consultation. A useful design brief
connects what matters for students with

01:34:42.355 --> 01:34:47.139
what we can change in the environment,
task or routine. It keeps individual

01:34:47.140 --> 01:34:51.405
knowledge in view without reducing a
person to their support needs.

01:34:52.920 --> 01:34:57.934
Here’s how the session runs. We’ll regroup
into trios, write a brief, and

01:34:57.935 --> 01:35:03.298
consider a short menu of options. Then
we’ll apply a universal design lens and

01:35:03.299 --> 01:35:08.801
finish one practical design change. After
that, each person will privately check

01:35:08.802 --> 01:35:13.677
the design against their own students.
That check will take us into the

01:35:13.678 --> 01:35:19.389
continuum of support. We’ll finish with a
gallery and a personal commitment. So the

01:35:19.390 --> 01:35:24.823
rhythm is short inputs, then doing. The
trio creates one design together, while

01:35:24.824 --> 01:35:30.326
each of you keeps your own students in
view. By the end, you’ll have one lesson,

01:35:30.327 --> 01:35:35.690
assessment task or handover redesigned,
checked against real students, and one

01:35:35.691 --> 01:35:40.984
thing to try. The recording has brief
pauses at the activity points; pause it

01:35:40.985 --> 01:35:44.188
for the full working time whenever you
need to.

01:35:45.720 --> 01:35:50.590
Find the Reduce item on your Carry into
design card. What does it point to: a

01:35:50.591 --> 01:35:55.332
lesson, an assessment task or a handover?
In the workshop room, move to the

01:35:55.333 --> 01:36:00.203
matching corner and form a trio with two
people nearby. Mixed subjects can be

01:36:00.204 --> 01:36:05.330
useful here, because support often needs
to work across the timetable. A handover

01:36:05.331 --> 01:36:09.752
is something that carries useful knowledge
from one person or place to

01:36:09.753 --> 01:36:14.559
another: between subject teachers, to a
relief teacher, from one year to the

01:36:14.560 --> 01:36:19.622
next, or between learning support and the
classroom. Each trio needs one A three

01:36:19.623 --> 01:36:24.621
sheet, and each person needs a private My
students card. Bring your day map and

01:36:24.622 --> 01:36:29.363
audit. Even though you will design
together, each of you keeps your own two

01:36:29.364 --> 01:36:34.234
or three students in mind. Allow a few
minutes to move and settle. If you are

01:36:34.235 --> 01:36:39.297
following independently, choose one of the
three design targets and work through

01:36:39.298 --> 01:36:42.886
it for your setting. Pause the video while
you get ready.

01:36:50.920 --> 01:36:56.982
Let’s settle into our groups. Check that
your trio has an A three sheet and that

01:36:56.983 --> 01:37:02.278
each of you has your own My students card.
You’ll agree on one lesson,

01:37:02.279 --> 01:37:08.494
assessment task or handover shortly. For
now, the shared design target gives you a

01:37:08.495 --> 01:37:14.557
place to begin; it doesn’t mean all your
students experience it in the same way.

01:37:16.080 --> 01:37:21.340
Before we begin, bring a concise picture
of your students back into view. Include

01:37:21.341 --> 01:37:26.601
their strengths, interests and sources of
joy. Think about communication, sensory

01:37:26.602 --> 01:37:31.665
experience and thinking; what costs and
what lightens; identity and belonging.

01:37:31.666 --> 01:37:36.335
Include what students, families and the
team already know, alongside the

01:37:36.336 --> 01:37:41.596
questions still open. Remember the quiet
student described as good and chilled in

01:37:41.597 --> 01:37:46.989
class. A calm appearance alone won’t tell
you whether a design is working for them.

01:37:46.990 --> 01:37:51.790
These are prompts to hold in mind, not
another form to complete. We’re not

01:37:51.791 --> 01:37:57.116
building a file on one young person, and
we’re not ranking students by need. We’re

01:37:57.117 --> 01:38:02.378
keeping real, different people in view as
we design. Later, in the private check,

01:38:02.379 --> 01:38:07.639
you’ll return to each of these students
and consider how the same design may work

01:38:07.640 --> 01:38:09.020
differently for them.

01:38:10.560 --> 01:38:15.553
Now agree on one lesson, assessment task
or handover your trio recognises. It

01:38:15.554 --> 01:38:20.941
might come from one person’s Reduce card.
Keep it specific enough to work on today.

01:38:20.942 --> 01:38:26.067
On section one of your A three sheet,
complete three sentences together. First:

01:38:26.068 --> 01:38:31.258
‘This asks students to...’ Describe what
the environment or task requires. Second:

01:38:31.259 --> 01:38:36.449
‘For some students, that means...’ Connect
the demand with what you know, and mark

01:38:36.450 --> 01:38:41.180
what is still a question. Third: ‘We could
change...’ Name something within

01:38:41.181 --> 01:38:46.043
educator or school influence. The same
prompts are on page eighteen of your

01:38:46.044 --> 01:38:50.708
Participant Guide. Check your wording
before you finish. Is it specific,

01:38:50.709 --> 01:38:55.702
workable and free from blame or deficit
labels? A phrase about what a student

01:38:55.703 --> 01:39:01.091
won’t do may need to become a description
of what the lesson, task or handover asks

01:39:01.092 --> 01:39:05.953
them to manage. Allow about eight minutes.
You’re writing one brief to keep

01:39:05.954 --> 01:39:10.619
active for the rest of this session. Pause
the video and work through it

01:39:10.620 --> 01:39:11.210
together.

01:39:19.240 --> 01:39:24.049
Let’s bring that first stage to a close.
Read your three sentences back to one

01:39:24.050 --> 01:39:28.733
another. Can you point to the demand in
the lesson, task or handover and the

01:39:28.734 --> 01:39:33.543
change within your influence? Have any
interpretations slipped in as facts? If

01:39:33.544 --> 01:39:38.290
you need to know more from a student,
family or colleague, keep that question

01:39:38.291 --> 01:39:43.412
visible. Your brief can be useful without
pretending that every part of the picture

01:39:43.413 --> 01:39:47.660
is settled. Leave it in front of you as we
look at possible supports.

01:39:49.200 --> 01:39:54.486
Here are four options to consider with
your brief in view: executive functioning

01:39:54.487 --> 01:39:59.774
meets assessment, predictability across
the timetable, supports that travel, and

01:39:59.775 --> 01:40:04.659
visual supports. The slide refers to
printed option cards, but no separate

01:40:04.660 --> 01:40:09.679
cards have been supplied with these
materials. You can use the four headings

01:40:09.680 --> 01:40:14.832
on screen and in the Options we chose
section on page eighteen instead. As you

01:40:14.833 --> 01:40:19.785
listen, keep returning to your first
sentence: this asks students to. Which

01:40:19.786 --> 01:40:24.537
option might answer that particular
demand? We’ll choose one or two. The

01:40:24.538 --> 01:40:29.423
value is in the match between the support
and the access question you have

01:40:29.424 --> 01:40:30.159
identified.

01:40:31.680 --> 01:40:36.702
Consider this example: a student who knows
the subject misses an assessment

01:40:36.703 --> 01:40:41.929
submission. Before assuming that they
didn’t care, what else would you want to

01:40:41.930 --> 01:40:46.545
understand? Think about starting,
planning, holding steps in mind and

01:40:46.546 --> 01:40:51.839
managing time. The four moves on this
slide make those demands clearer. Publish

01:40:51.840 --> 01:40:56.591
assessment schedules early. Break tasks
into smaller parts with visible

01:40:56.592 --> 01:41:01.614
milestones. Show an exemplar of the
finished thing so students can see what

01:41:01.615 --> 01:41:07.180
they are working towards. Make submission
steps literal: where does the work go, in

01:41:07.181 --> 01:41:12.406
what format, and by when? If a student
needs help holding information in mind,

01:41:12.407 --> 01:41:17.226
put useful information where they can
return to it. Written first steps,

01:41:17.227 --> 01:41:22.385
instructions that stay visible and a clear
clock or timer are examples. Match

01:41:22.386 --> 01:41:27.272
them to the demand and check with students
whether they help. Think about

01:41:27.273 --> 01:41:32.363
homework too. How much of the task depends
on organisation and time, as well

01:41:32.364 --> 01:41:37.725
as subject knowledge? An unfinished task
may leave questions about access that a

01:41:37.726 --> 01:41:43.156
mark alone won’t answer. These supports
help students organise themselves to show

01:41:43.157 --> 01:41:48.450
what they know. Keep the learning purpose
clear and involve the relevant school

01:41:48.451 --> 01:41:53.541
staff where assessment policy or formal
adjustments sit beyond one teacher’s

01:41:53.542 --> 01:41:58.564
decision. Return to your brief: which
demand could you make more visible or

01:41:58.565 --> 01:41:59.311
manageable?

01:42:00.840 --> 01:42:06.898
Predictability means having accessible
information about routine and change. It

01:42:06.899 --> 01:42:13.268
doesn’t mean every day must be identical,
or that we can promise nothing unexpected

01:42:13.269 --> 01:42:18.939
will happen. Think about how much
information in a secondary day is spoken

01:42:18.940 --> 01:42:24.920
once, implied, or held by adults. It may
change between periods. If I couldn’t

01:42:24.921 --> 01:42:30.824
rely on remembering what someone told me,
how would I know what was happening

01:42:30.825 --> 01:42:36.106
across today’s timetable? A useful support
helps students answer four

01:42:36.107 --> 01:42:42.165
questions: what is happening, what is
changing, what comes next, and what can I

01:42:42.166 --> 01:42:48.457
do if the plan changes? Give information
early enough to be useful, in every class

01:42:48.458 --> 01:42:54.672
and on relief days. Imagine a Year Nine
student arrives at period three to find a

01:42:54.673 --> 01:43:00.420
relief teacher, another room and a changed
task. Could they find out before

01:43:00.421 --> 01:43:05.858
arriving? Is the change available
somewhere they can check, such as the

01:43:05.859 --> 01:43:11.451
class page or timetable app? Does the
relief plan keep the usual starting

01:43:11.452 --> 01:43:16.733
routine? Can the student ask for
information or support in a way that

01:43:16.734 --> 01:43:22.870
works for them? Return to the day audit.
When something changes, how do students

01:43:22.871 --> 01:43:29.162
know? Consistent routines, warning before
transitions and information in more than

01:43:29.163 --> 01:43:35.066
one form can help. Review the support
through actual experience and feedback.

01:43:36.600 --> 01:43:40.868
Let’s return to six classrooms, six
teachers, one student. A support that

01:43:40.868 --> 01:43:45.669
works in one period may be unknown in the
next. This option is about making useful

01:43:45.669 --> 01:43:50.114
guidance travel. The slide describes a
short, one-page profile of strengths,

01:43:50.114 --> 01:43:54.738
interests, what helps and what costs. It
is written with the student and shared

01:43:54.738 --> 01:43:59.243
with the teachers who need it, including
relief teachers. The student remains

01:43:59.243 --> 01:44:03.925
involved in what is written and shared.
The example image is still a placeholder

01:44:03.925 --> 01:44:08.608
in this deck, so focus on the questions
rather than a finished template. Would a

01:44:08.608 --> 01:44:12.698
relief teacher, or the period four
teacher, know what helps before the

01:44:12.698 --> 01:44:17.321
lesson starts? Where would that guidance
live so it is actually seen? Who would

01:44:17.321 --> 01:44:21.885
update it, and how would the student
contribute? Separate useful guidance from

01:44:21.885 --> 01:44:26.686
unnecessary personal detail. A page that
gets lost in a shared drive won’t provide

01:44:26.686 --> 01:44:30.954
continuity. A clear place, a known
updating process and the student’s own

01:44:30.954 --> 01:44:35.399
perspective can make it more useful. Think
back to your communication notes:

01:44:35.399 --> 01:44:39.608
what should travel between people and
places? That question belongs in a

01:44:39.608 --> 01:44:41.800
handover as much as in a lesson plan.

01:44:43.320 --> 01:44:48.414
With any visual support, start by asking
what the student needs to find,

01:44:48.415 --> 01:44:53.653
understand, remember or communicate. Then
choose a form that can make that

01:44:53.654 --> 01:44:58.964
information clear. It might be an object,
photograph, symbol, written word,

01:44:58.965 --> 01:45:04.203
checklist, map or choice support. In
secondary, that might be instructions

01:45:04.204 --> 01:45:09.729
that stay on the board or class page, a
task map, a checklist or an assessment

01:45:09.730 --> 01:45:14.896
calendar. Choose a format that is
respectful and suits the young person’s

01:45:14.897 --> 01:45:20.063
age and preferences. A support available
to the whole class may feel more

01:45:20.064 --> 01:45:24.584
comfortable than one that singles a
student out. Visual does not

01:45:24.585 --> 01:45:29.536
automatically mean accessible. The student
needs to understand what it

01:45:29.537 --> 01:45:35.062
represents and be able to use it at the
point of need. Keep unnecessary detail

01:45:35.063 --> 01:45:40.731
out, explain how it works, and check its
meaning with the student. Page nineteen

01:45:40.732 --> 01:45:45.970
gives four quick-review questions. Can the
student understand it? Can they

01:45:45.971 --> 01:45:51.065
access it when they need it? Does it
reflect what is actually happening,

01:45:51.066 --> 01:45:56.376
including change? Does it give useful
information or a way to respond? Keep

01:45:56.377 --> 01:46:01.399
understanding and expressing in view. A
visual support may help someone

01:46:01.400 --> 01:46:07.212
understand, while A A C provides ways to
communicate. Access to A A C doesn’t mean

01:46:07.213 --> 01:46:12.164
every spoken instruction or schedule is
understood. Keep the student’s

01:46:12.165 --> 01:46:17.690
communication system available in every
class, during transitions and changes,

01:46:17.691 --> 01:46:23.288
and recognise responses beyond speech. The
materials refer to a Visual Supports

01:46:23.289 --> 01:46:28.239
Checklist and an online webinar for
further learning, but their access

01:46:28.240 --> 01:46:33.694
details still need to be confirmed. The
review questions on page nineteen are

01:46:33.695 --> 01:46:39.076
ready to use. A checklist supports
personalisation and consultation; it does

01:46:39.077 --> 01:46:44.458
not prescribe one format for every
student. Keep asking: does this give this

01:46:44.459 --> 01:46:49.123
student useful information they can
understand, access and act on?

01:46:50.641 --> 01:46:55.937
Return to your brief and the four option
headings. Which one or two options answer

01:46:55.938 --> 01:46:59.927
it? You’ve heard about executive
functioning meets assessment,

01:46:59.928 --> 01:47:04.962
predictability across the timetable,
supports that travel and visual supports.

01:47:04.963 --> 01:47:09.998
Sort those possibilities against the
demand in your design. Choose one or two.

01:47:09.999 --> 01:47:15.034
For each, write down why it fits: which
specific demand does it answer? Record

01:47:15.035 --> 01:47:20.397
the choice and your reason on section two
of the A three sheet. You can also follow

01:47:20.398 --> 01:47:24.844
the prompts on page eighteen. Give
yourselves about three minutes. If

01:47:24.845 --> 01:47:30.010
everything seems useful, return to the
brief and narrow the choice. We’re making

01:47:30.011 --> 01:47:34.850
a manageable response to a known demand.
Pause the video for your decision.

01:47:42.880 --> 01:47:48.011
Let’s come back. Check that you have a
reason beside each option. Saying that a

01:47:48.012 --> 01:47:53.078
support is often helpful is different from
saying what it will make clearer or

01:47:53.079 --> 01:47:57.553
easier in this design. Keep that
connection visible. It will help you

01:47:57.554 --> 01:48:02.224
explain the design, decide what to
observe, and revise the support if it

01:48:02.225 --> 01:48:04.659
doesn’t help in the way you expected.

01:48:06.160 --> 01:48:11.380
Before you finish the design, let’s apply
a Universal Design for Learning lens. Can

01:48:11.381 --> 01:48:16.538
you make important information clear and
available? Can you offer workable ways to

01:48:16.539 --> 01:48:21.313
participate and respond? Can you support
engagement while keeping meaningful

01:48:21.314 --> 01:48:26.343
challenge in the learning? Knowledge of
individual students and universal design

01:48:26.344 --> 01:48:31.246
can inform each other. Something you
understand about one student’s access may

01:48:31.247 --> 01:48:36.085
reveal a barrier worth reducing more
broadly. A broadly available support may

01:48:36.086 --> 01:48:40.860
then help several students in different
ways. But the presence of a resource

01:48:40.861 --> 01:48:45.636
doesn’t guarantee access. Offering another
format only helps if students can

01:48:45.637 --> 01:48:50.539
find it, understand it and use it. We’ll
now examine your emerging design with

01:48:50.540 --> 01:48:54.550
four practical questions, then test it
through use and feedback.

01:48:56.080 --> 01:49:01.596
Use section three of your A three sheet,
with the prompts on page nineteen. Run

01:49:01.597 --> 01:49:06.901
four questions over your draft design. Can
students find and understand what

01:49:06.902 --> 01:49:12.136
they need? Can they take part in more than
one way? Can they pause, ask for

01:49:12.137 --> 01:49:17.511
help or recover? Is the support available
when demand increases? Consider the

01:49:17.512 --> 01:49:22.533
details. If help is only available by
asking aloud, is that workable for

01:49:22.534 --> 01:49:27.414
everyone using this routine? If
information is useful at the start but

01:49:27.415 --> 01:49:32.719
disappears during a transition, will it be
there when needed? Would the same

01:49:32.720 --> 01:49:37.812
access to participation, help, pause and
recovery remain available with a

01:49:37.813 --> 01:49:43.258
different teacher or on a relief day?
These are questions to test against your

01:49:43.259 --> 01:49:48.493
setting, rather than assumptions about a
student. Tick each question as you

01:49:48.494 --> 01:49:54.010
consider it. Where the answer is ‘not
yet’, adjust the design before moving on.

01:49:54.011 --> 01:49:58.890
Allow about six minutes. Pause the video
while your trio completes the

01:49:58.891 --> 01:50:00.305
accessibility check.

01:50:08.320 --> 01:50:13.112
Come back to the changes you made. The
check should influence the design, not

01:50:13.113 --> 01:50:17.149
just record that you discussed
accessibility. Keep any unresolved

01:50:17.150 --> 01:50:21.942
question beside the plan. This is an
initial check. You’ll still need to find

01:50:21.943 --> 01:50:26.861
out what happens when students use the
support and have a way to give feedback.

01:50:26.862 --> 01:50:31.339
Access is demonstrated through experience,
rather than guaranteed by the

01:50:31.340 --> 01:50:32.979
resource we have prepared.

01:50:34.480 --> 01:50:39.476
Now finish one practical change on section
four of your A three sheet. Page

01:50:39.477 --> 01:50:44.608
twenty of the Participant Guide carries
the design prompts. Work through five

01:50:44.609 --> 01:50:49.943
steps. State the barrier or unnecessary
demand. Describe what will change in the

01:50:49.944 --> 01:50:55.075
environment, task or routine. Include
students’ communication and experience.

01:50:55.076 --> 01:51:00.275
Decide what you will observe. Then set a
time to review. Keep the change small

01:51:00.276 --> 01:51:05.137
enough to try. You might change
information, timing, materials or the way

01:51:05.138 --> 01:51:10.404
the routine is organised, depending on
your brief. Be clear about what you hope

01:51:10.405 --> 01:51:15.536
students will be able to access or do more
readily. The measure of success is

01:51:15.537 --> 01:51:20.803
access, rather than compliance. For a
handover or assessment task, ask who else

01:51:20.804 --> 01:51:25.598
needs to know about the change: other
subject teachers, relief teachers,

01:51:25.599 --> 01:51:30.460
learning support or the year level
coordinator. If a decision sits beyond

01:51:30.461 --> 01:51:35.929
one classroom, note where it needs to go.
Near the end, ask a neighbouring trio to

01:51:35.930 --> 01:51:40.319
check that your design remains
student-centred and focused on what

01:51:40.320 --> 01:51:45.316
educators can change. If you’re working
alone, read it again from those two

01:51:45.317 --> 01:51:50.043
perspectives. Prepare one sentence
describing the change and the access

01:51:50.044 --> 01:51:54.905
question it addresses. Allow about ten
minutes. Pause the video while you

01:51:54.906 --> 01:51:56.863
develop and check the design.

01:52:04.880 --> 01:52:09.522
Let’s finish the sentence you’re writing
and come back together. You should have a

01:52:09.523 --> 01:52:13.878
change you can describe, something to
observe, and a time to review it. Check

01:52:13.879 --> 01:52:18.406
that student communication and experience
are included in how you will judge it.

01:52:18.407 --> 01:52:22.992
Hold onto your one-sentence description.
Now we’re going to see what happens when

01:52:22.993 --> 01:52:27.291
one shared design meets the different
students each of you has been thinking

01:52:27.292 --> 01:52:27.635
about.

01:52:29.160 --> 01:52:34.231
This next part is private. Take your own
My students card and turn to page

01:52:34.232 --> 01:52:39.165
twenty-one if you’re using the Participant
Guide. Use Student A, Student

01:52:39.166 --> 01:52:44.307
B and Student C, without names or
identifying details. Consider your trio’s

01:52:44.308 --> 01:52:49.796
design against each student. Mark Helps,
No difference, or Needs something more.

01:52:49.797 --> 01:52:55.007
Treat this as a working judgement based on
what you know, and keep questions

01:52:55.008 --> 01:53:00.149
visible where you need the student’s or
family’s perspective. Between three

01:53:00.150 --> 01:53:05.500
people, a trio may be holding six to nine
different students in view. The same

01:53:05.501 --> 01:53:11.058
design may work differently for each. You
can share only what you choose, perhaps

01:53:11.059 --> 01:53:16.548
how many Needs something more marks the
design collected, without describing the

01:53:16.549 --> 01:53:21.690
students. Allow about seven minutes for
your private check and any optional

01:53:21.691 --> 01:53:24.469
sharing. Pause the video while you work.

01:53:32.480 --> 01:53:37.284
Let’s return to the shared design. Where
it helps, the universal approach is doing

01:53:37.285 --> 01:53:41.199
useful work. Where it makes no difference,
or where a student needs

01:53:41.200 --> 01:53:45.944
something more, we have an important next
question. That doesn’t make the student

01:53:45.945 --> 01:53:50.690
the problem, and it doesn’t necessarily
make the whole design a failure. It shows

01:53:50.691 --> 01:53:54.961
us where targeted support or an individual
adjustment may be needed. Keep

01:53:54.962 --> 01:53:59.647
those Needs something more marks in mind
as we look at the continuum of support.

01:54:01.160 --> 01:54:05.999
Support is a continuum, rather than one
intervention that answers every need.

01:54:06.000 --> 01:54:10.584
Start with an inclusive environment:
predictable routines across classes,

01:54:10.585 --> 01:54:15.487
clear expectations, assessment schedules
published early, and a school culture

01:54:15.488 --> 01:54:20.263
where different ways of participating are
accepted and valued. Then consider

01:54:20.264 --> 01:54:25.103
universal supports available to many
students. Instructions can be offered in

01:54:25.104 --> 01:54:29.369
spoken, written and visual forms. Students
may have flexible ways to

01:54:29.370 --> 01:54:34.527
demonstrate learning, with exemplars and a
class page where task information stays

01:54:34.528 --> 01:54:39.685
available. Targeted support responds to a
more specific need. For example, a small

01:54:39.686 --> 01:54:44.780
group might benefit from key vocabulary
being introduced before a lesson, or from

01:54:44.781 --> 01:54:49.365
check-ins on assessment milestones.
Individual adjustments respond to the

01:54:49.366 --> 01:54:53.568
particular student’s access and
participation needs. Examples might

01:54:53.569 --> 01:54:58.344
include a quieter workspace for an
assessment, additional processing time, a

01:54:58.345 --> 01:55:03.056
personalised task breakdown or another way
to demonstrate learning. What is

01:55:03.057 --> 01:55:08.087
appropriate depends on the student and the
context, informed by consultation and

01:55:08.088 --> 01:55:13.245
evidence. Look back at the design check.
What is available to everyone? What might

01:55:13.246 --> 01:55:17.193
need to be targeted? What needs individual
adjustment? Academic

01:55:17.194 --> 01:55:21.778
adjustments sit within this wider picture.
A diagnosis alone doesn’t tell

01:55:21.779 --> 01:55:26.745
us which adjustment will help. Connect the
response with your observations, the

01:55:26.746 --> 01:55:31.330
day map, and student and family knowledge,
then review it through use and

01:55:31.331 --> 01:55:31.902
feedback.

01:55:33.440 --> 01:55:38.428
Reasonable adjustments are changes or
supports that help students access and

01:55:38.429 --> 01:55:42.951
participate in education in a way that
works for them. They should be

01:55:42.952 --> 01:55:47.740
individualised, build on strengths, remove
barriers, and be practical and

01:55:47.741 --> 01:55:52.596
effective. Informed decisions and review
help us find out whether they are

01:55:52.597 --> 01:55:57.651
enabling the student. The diagram places
adjustments within the wider work of

01:55:57.652 --> 01:56:03.039
environmental design and Universal Design
for Learning. It also names the four N C

01:56:03.040 --> 01:56:07.429
C D levels of adjustment: quality
differentiated teaching practice,

01:56:07.430 --> 01:56:12.551
supplementary, substantial and extensive.
Keep two things distinct. Reasonable

01:56:12.552 --> 01:56:17.873
adjustments are the changes and supports
provided. The N C C D is the process for

01:56:17.874 --> 01:56:23.261
collecting information about adjustments
for students with disability. Evidence of

01:56:23.262 --> 01:56:28.116
what has been provided and monitored
matters; a diagnostic label by itself

01:56:28.117 --> 01:56:33.438
doesn’t choose an adjustment or tell the
whole story. Within curriculum learning,

01:56:33.439 --> 01:56:37.761
we want support to build capability,
confidence, self-advocacy and

01:56:37.762 --> 01:56:42.950
independence. The question is whether
students can access and participate, with

01:56:42.951 --> 01:56:48.072
support that empowers them. For formal
requirements and decisions, use current

01:56:48.073 --> 01:56:51.597
official guidance and your school or
system processes.

01:56:53.120 --> 01:56:57.997
For N C C D information, use the current
official guidance alongside your school

01:56:57.998 --> 01:57:02.011
or system’s consultation, documentation
and review processes. Make

01:57:02.012 --> 01:57:06.827
responsibilities clear to the student,
family and staff involved: what is being

01:57:06.828 --> 01:57:11.458
tried, who will put it in place, what
evidence will be gathered, and when it

01:57:11.459 --> 01:57:15.904
will be reviewed. Keep the connection to
the educational access question.

01:57:15.905 --> 01:57:20.721
Documentation should help explain the
student’s needs, the adjustments provided

01:57:20.722 --> 01:57:25.475
and what is learned from reviewing them.
It shouldn’t replace listening to the

01:57:25.476 --> 01:57:29.983
student or family. Today’s design work
gives you a starting point for that

01:57:29.984 --> 01:57:34.429
conversation. It doesn’t replace the
formal processes in your setting. In

01:57:34.430 --> 01:57:39.307
Session Five we’ll develop consultation
and partnership further, including the N

01:57:39.308 --> 01:57:43.506
C C D meeting tool kit, so keep your open
questions with your design.

01:57:45.040 --> 01:57:49.968
It’s time to see what other trios have
designed. Put your A three sheet on the

01:57:49.969 --> 01:57:54.898
wall, checking first that it contains no
student names or identifying details.

01:57:54.899 --> 01:57:59.891
Walk through the gallery and read a few
other designs. On another trio’s sheet,

01:57:59.892 --> 01:58:04.500
leave a Post-it with one idea you would
borrow or one question. Keep your

01:58:04.501 --> 01:58:09.301
question curious and useful: something
that might help the group clarify the

01:58:09.302 --> 01:58:14.167
access question or refine the change. Then
return to your own design and read

01:58:14.168 --> 01:58:18.584
the notes others have left. Allow about
six minutes. If you’re working

01:58:18.585 --> 01:58:23.449
independently, use this point to ask a
colleague for that same brief response

01:58:23.450 --> 01:58:26.650
when you can. Pause the video for the
gallery walk.

01:58:34.680 --> 01:58:39.618
Come back to your own design. Read the
feedback and consider whether you want to

01:58:39.619 --> 01:58:44.431
refine anything. You don’t need to adopt
every suggestion; test it against the

01:58:44.432 --> 01:58:48.995
brief and the students’ access. The
gallery lets useful ideas travel, much

01:58:48.996 --> 01:58:53.684
like our communication wall in Session
Three. An idea from another group can

01:58:53.685 --> 01:58:58.560
open a possibility, while still needing to
fit the students and routine in your

01:58:58.561 --> 01:58:59.060
setting.

01:59:00.560 --> 01:59:06.056
To finish, open the Commit activity on
page twenty-one. This has three places

01:59:06.057 --> 01:59:11.914
for your next steps. First, this week in
my own classes. What is one thing you can

01:59:11.915 --> 01:59:17.411
actually try? Second, with my faculty or
year level team. What needs a shared

01:59:17.412 --> 01:59:22.980
approach? Third, to raise with school
leaders. What requires a decision beyond

01:59:22.981 --> 01:59:28.332
your classroom? Assessment policy,
timetabling and reporting can sit beyond

01:59:28.333 --> 01:59:33.973
one teacher’s influence. Naming the right
level for a change helps it reach the

01:59:33.974 --> 01:59:39.543
right people. Keep the this-week box
practical too; there may be something you

01:59:39.544 --> 01:59:44.605
can do while a wider question is
considered. Then complete who you will

01:59:44.606 --> 01:59:49.741
involve, including the student and family,
and how students and families

01:59:49.742 --> 01:59:54.948
will help refine the design. If you
prefer, the bottom of your private My

01:59:54.949 --> 02:00:00.083
students card also has space for one thing
you will try and who you will

02:00:00.084 --> 02:00:05.001
involve. Allow about four minutes. Sharing
is optional and any public

02:00:05.002 --> 02:00:10.498
example stays anonymous. Pause the video
now while you record the commitment.

02:00:18.520 --> 02:00:23.884
Let’s close by looking at what you have:
one lesson, assessment task or handover

02:00:23.885 --> 02:00:28.910
examined, one change designed, and a next
step that includes other people’s

02:00:28.911 --> 02:00:33.867
knowledge. Treat this as something to try,
review and refine. Students and

02:00:33.868 --> 02:00:39.029
families may help us see something our day
map missed, or show that a support

02:00:39.030 --> 02:00:44.394
works differently from what we expected.
That is useful information for the next

02:00:44.395 --> 02:00:49.284
version of the design. Your this-week
change can become your Start on the

02:00:49.285 --> 02:00:54.309
action card in Session Six. In Session
Five, we’ll move from this classroom

02:00:54.310 --> 02:00:58.860
design work into home-school relationships
and positive partnership,

02:00:58.861 --> 02:01:00.965
with the student at the centre.

02:01:09.000 --> 02:01:13.934
Welcome to Working in Partnership. In the
last session, you chose something you

02:01:13.934 --> 02:01:18.615
could change in a learning environment.
Now we're going to consider how the

02:01:18.615 --> 02:01:23.802
student, family and school can understand
that experience together, and decide what

02:01:23.802 --> 02:01:28.420
happens next. Have your My students card
handy, on page twenty-one of your

02:01:28.420 --> 02:01:33.227
Participant Guide. Look at the line, who I
will involve. That is our starting

02:01:33.227 --> 02:01:38.098
point. You may be bringing examples of
partnerships that have been productive,

02:01:38.098 --> 02:01:43.158
and others that have been difficult. Both
can help us think. Please keep students

02:01:43.158 --> 02:01:47.966
and families anonymous, using initials or
roles, and share only what you feel

02:01:47.966 --> 02:01:53.026
comfortable sharing. The student stays at
the centre. Partnership begins while we

02:01:53.026 --> 02:01:57.644
are developing our understanding, and
continues through design, action and

02:01:57.644 --> 02:01:58.086
review.

02:01:59.600 --> 02:02:04.595
Look at the overlapping circles. Family,
school staff and support staff each bring

02:02:04.596 --> 02:02:09.468
knowledge and a different relationship
with the student. The student is right at

02:02:09.469 --> 02:02:13.910
the centre. A family may understand
something about recovery after school

02:02:13.911 --> 02:02:18.782
that we don't see in the classroom. An
educator may notice how a particular task

02:02:18.783 --> 02:02:23.779
changes participation. Support staff may
see a student across more of the day than

02:02:23.780 --> 02:02:28.097
any one subject teacher and can contribute
a perspective we need to ask

02:02:28.098 --> 02:02:32.846
for. With several subject teachers, a year
level coordinator, learning support

02:02:32.847 --> 02:02:37.781
and sometimes relief staff involved, that
knowledge is spread across many people.

02:02:37.782 --> 02:02:42.654
Those are possible contributions, rather
than fixed roles. No one person has the

02:02:42.655 --> 02:02:46.663
whole picture, including us. On page
twenty-two, you'll find Where

02:02:46.664 --> 02:02:51.598
partnerships converge. With someone near
you, consider this question: what is the

02:02:51.599 --> 02:02:56.533
goal of these intersecting partnerships?
Then ask, who might be missing from this

02:02:56.534 --> 02:03:01.467
picture? How will the student communicate
their experience and preferences? Think

02:03:01.468 --> 02:03:05.970
back to sensory and thinking in Session
Two, communication, connection and

02:03:05.971 --> 02:03:10.720
identity in Session Three, and learning
design and the continuum of support in

02:03:10.721 --> 02:03:14.729
Session Four. What should these
partnerships make possible for the

02:03:14.730 --> 02:03:19.479
student? Pause the video if you'd like
longer to talk, and then we'll draw the

02:03:19.480 --> 02:03:20.404
ideas together.

02:03:28.440 --> 02:03:33.346
Let's come back to the student at the
centre. A useful shared purpose connects

02:03:33.346 --> 02:03:37.870
our work to their learning, access,
participation and experience. Having

02:03:37.870 --> 02:03:41.884
several people involved doesn't
automatically make a partnership

02:03:41.884 --> 02:03:46.663
effective. Their knowledge needs a way
into the decisions. If you identified

02:03:46.663 --> 02:03:51.251
someone missing, keep that in mind. If the
student's perspective is being

02:03:51.251 --> 02:03:56.348
inferred entirely by adults, consider how
you could check that understanding with

02:03:56.348 --> 02:04:00.999
them. The circles are prompts for thinking
about relationships. They can't

02:04:00.999 --> 02:04:05.778
capture every person or every kind of
knowledge that matters to a particular

02:04:05.778 --> 02:04:06.288
student.

02:04:07.800 --> 02:04:12.308
These six principles come from the
Family-School Partnerships Framework.

02:04:12.309 --> 02:04:16.626
Let's connect each one to what we do.
First, parents and families are

02:04:16.627 --> 02:04:20.753
children's first and continuing educators.
Their knowledge remains

02:04:20.754 --> 02:04:25.580
valuable when a child enters school.
Second, learning is lifelong and happens

02:04:25.581 --> 02:04:30.787
across many settings. The classroom gives
us an important view, but it is one view.

02:04:30.788 --> 02:04:35.867
Third, partnerships are strengthened when
the diversity and strengths of families

02:04:35.868 --> 02:04:40.947
are valued. We need to learn what those
strengths are, rather than assuming every

02:04:40.948 --> 02:04:45.837
family can contribute in the same way.
Fourth, community engagement can expand

02:04:45.838 --> 02:04:50.663
the people, knowledge and resources
involved in learning. Fifth, partnerships

02:04:50.664 --> 02:04:55.171
grow through mutual trust, respect and
responsibility. We can show those

02:04:55.172 --> 02:04:59.553
qualities by listening carefully,
explaining clearly and doing what we

02:04:59.554 --> 02:05:04.252
agree to do. Sixth, partnership needs
committed, collaborative and creative

02:05:04.253 --> 02:05:08.570
leadership. Someone needs to make the time
and processes possible. In

02:05:08.571 --> 02:05:12.952
secondary, families may have fewer
informal contacts with teachers and

02:05:12.953 --> 02:05:17.651
several people to reach. Who is the
family’s main point of contact? How can

02:05:17.652 --> 02:05:22.414
useful communication happen before the
next report or problem? In a meeting,

02:05:22.415 --> 02:05:27.430
these principles become visible through a
shared purpose, student communication,

02:05:27.431 --> 02:05:32.447
accessible information, and clear next
steps. Who will do what, and when will we

02:05:32.448 --> 02:05:36.956
check in again? Family and school
perspectives may differ. We can listen

02:05:36.957 --> 02:05:41.337
and show respect while we are still
working through those differences.

02:05:41.338 --> 02:05:46.544
Bringing those perspectives together from
the beginning gives us a better basis for

02:05:46.545 --> 02:05:47.878
deciding what to try.

02:05:49.400 --> 02:05:54.429
There are many points of collaboration
around an Autistic student: between

02:05:54.430 --> 02:05:59.804
teachers, with a family, with support
staff, with other professionals, and with

02:05:59.805 --> 02:06:04.972
the student themselves. Across those
relationships, the core skills are much

02:06:04.973 --> 02:06:09.726
the same. Before we work through the ideas
on the slide, think about a

02:06:09.727 --> 02:06:15.238
collaboration that helped you understand
something better. What made it possible?

02:06:15.239 --> 02:06:20.475
Perhaps there was a clear purpose, time to
ask questions, or a willingness to

02:06:20.476 --> 02:06:25.643
change an initial view. At your table,
name two or three principles that you

02:06:25.644 --> 02:06:31.087
think matter most. You can also consider
the question on the slide: how could we

02:06:31.088 --> 02:06:35.703
work together for better learning
outcomes? Keep the student in that

02:06:35.704 --> 02:06:40.250
conversation. Pause the video for a longer
discussion, then resume.

02:06:48.280 --> 02:06:53.240
Let's bring those principles together.
Shared goals help us understand what

02:06:53.241 --> 02:06:58.671
we're working towards, and shared values
help us understand why it matters. Mutual

02:06:58.672 --> 02:07:03.901
trust and respect need to be present in
the way we speak and listen. Consulting

02:07:03.902 --> 02:07:09.331
on decisions means leaving room for what
we hear to influence the decision. Seeing

02:07:09.332 --> 02:07:13.957
another perspective and inviting curiosity
help us notice what our own

02:07:13.958 --> 02:07:18.986
position might hide. Centring student
voice reminds us whose experience this

02:07:18.987 --> 02:07:24.148
work concerns. Assuming good intent gives
us a constructive starting point. We

02:07:24.149 --> 02:07:29.110
still need to attend to the effects of our
actions, and to concerns someone

02:07:29.111 --> 02:07:34.607
raises. A useful question is, what can we
learn from each other that will help this

02:07:34.608 --> 02:07:38.295
student? These principles apply whoever is
at the table.

02:07:39.800 --> 02:07:44.762
The seesaw invites us to notice power
between school, home and the student.

02:07:44.763 --> 02:07:49.860
Power can sit in income and resources,
past experiences of education, culture

02:07:49.861 --> 02:07:54.891
and language, or knowledge of school
systems and curriculum. It can also sit

02:07:54.892 --> 02:07:59.988
in what each person believes about school
and professional authority. We work

02:07:59.989 --> 02:08:05.153
within education every day. Its language
and routines can feel ordinary to us,

02:08:05.154 --> 02:08:10.586
while being unfamiliar or intimidating to
someone else. Think about an agenda full

02:08:10.587 --> 02:08:15.750
of acronyms, a meeting time that is hard
to attend, or a decision presented as

02:08:15.751 --> 02:08:21.183
already settled. Think privately about a
meeting that was difficult. Who chose the

02:08:21.184 --> 02:08:26.549
room, time and agenda? Partnership works
both ways, and difficult experiences can

02:08:26.550 --> 02:08:31.781
affect everyone involved. Some families
also bring a history of school meetings

02:08:31.782 --> 02:08:37.013
with them. We can acknowledge that while
looking at what the school can change.

02:08:37.014 --> 02:08:41.842
Ask yourself: who sets the agenda? Whose
knowledge is heard? Who can make

02:08:41.843 --> 02:08:47.074
decisions? Is the student included in a
way that works for them? And what could

02:08:47.075 --> 02:08:52.238
the school change about the process? These
questions help us examine practice.

02:08:52.239 --> 02:08:57.470
They aren't an invitation to assign blame
to an individual. Before describing a

02:08:57.471 --> 02:09:02.501
family as difficult or disengaged, we need
to look carefully at the context,

02:09:02.502 --> 02:09:07.800
the relationship and the opportunities
they have been offered to contribute. Our

02:09:07.801 --> 02:09:11.890
intention matters, and so does how the
process is experienced.

02:09:13.400 --> 02:09:19.026
Let's explore that on page twenty-three,
under Power dynamics. You'll need three

02:09:19.027 --> 02:09:24.795
sticky notes, or three spaces on a page.
On the first, write an example of a power

02:09:24.796 --> 02:09:30.422
imbalance from a home perspective. On the
second, write an example from a school

02:09:30.423 --> 02:09:35.906
perspective. On the third, write how those
imbalances could be addressed. Keep

02:09:35.907 --> 02:09:40.963
the people involved anonymous. You might
think about language, access to

02:09:40.964 --> 02:09:46.376
information, who attends a meeting, or who
gets to define the problem. School

02:09:46.377 --> 02:09:52.145
acronyms such as NAPLAN, NCCD and IEP are
familiar to many educators, but we can't

02:09:52.146 --> 02:09:57.701
assume they are equally accessible to
families. Subject selection, pathways and

02:09:57.702 --> 02:10:03.185
senior certificate requirements may need
explanation too. Professional status,

02:10:03.186 --> 02:10:08.812
cultural expectations and the involvement
of external providers can also shape a

02:10:08.813 --> 02:10:14.011
meeting. Share your notes with your table.
If you would welcome a movement

02:10:14.012 --> 02:10:19.139
break, you could share with someone
elsewhere in the room, or place notes

02:10:19.140 --> 02:10:24.196
under Home, School and Solutions on a
wall. The live activity allows six

02:10:24.197 --> 02:10:29.538
minutes. Pause here to use that time. Look
for something that could make the

02:10:29.539 --> 02:10:32.671
process fairer, and resume when you're
ready.

02:10:40.680 --> 02:10:45.215
Let's come back together. Look at the
changes you've proposed through four

02:10:45.216 --> 02:10:49.503
questions. Who sets the agenda? Whose
knowledge is heard? Who can make

02:10:49.504 --> 02:10:53.729
decisions? What can the school change
about the process? Power can be

02:10:53.730 --> 02:10:58.079
structural, historical and cultural. It
can also operate without anyone

02:10:58.080 --> 02:11:03.051
consciously intending it. A difference in
status may feel significant even when a

02:11:03.052 --> 02:11:07.587
meeting has been organised with good
intentions. Choose one process change

02:11:07.588 --> 02:11:12.497
from your notes. It could be a change to
preparation, communication, the meeting

02:11:12.498 --> 02:11:17.530
itself or follow-up. Keep it beside your
student planning. We'll carry it into the

02:11:17.531 --> 02:11:22.253
bridge reflection at the end of this
session, and then into your conversation

02:11:22.254 --> 02:11:23.496
plan in Session Six.

02:11:25.000 --> 02:11:30.367
We're going to hear an example involving
Adon, where decisions were made without

02:11:30.368 --> 02:11:35.328
the student being listened to or
consulted. As you watch, pay attention to

02:11:35.329 --> 02:11:40.356
what decisions were made, and who made
them. Then consider the impact those

02:11:40.357 --> 02:11:45.384
decisions had on Adon. There's space to
reflect under Student voice on page

02:11:45.385 --> 02:11:47.150
twenty-three. Let's watch.

02:13:39.600 --> 02:13:44.420
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon? Take a moment to consider what you

02:13:44.421 --> 02:13:49.302
heard, and what might have been different
if Adon's perspective had been included

02:13:49.303 --> 02:13:54.063
in the decisions. Now bring the question
back to the students you have in mind.

02:13:54.064 --> 02:13:58.762
How are their preferences asked for and
heard? Is there a way for a student to

02:13:58.763 --> 02:14:03.705
tell you that something isn't working, as
well as to choose between options adults

02:14:03.706 --> 02:14:08.160
have already offered? Pause here if you'd
like time to reflect or discuss.

02:14:16.160 --> 02:14:20.508
Students need a say in decisions about
them, in ways that work with their

02:14:20.509 --> 02:14:25.159
communication. Questions such as, what
helps, what is hard, and what would you

02:14:25.160 --> 02:14:29.930
like teachers to know, can open up that
understanding. We may need to change the

02:14:29.931 --> 02:14:34.460
format, setting or time we allow for a
response. Where a student uses words,

02:14:34.461 --> 02:14:39.171
record their words accurately. Where they
communicate in other ways, make clear

02:14:39.172 --> 02:14:43.218
what they communicated and how. Keep adult
interpretation visible as

02:14:43.219 --> 02:14:47.869
interpretation, and let the student’s
perspective influence what happens next.

02:14:47.870 --> 02:14:52.399
For adolescents, being asked can lead into
taking part in their own meetings

02:14:52.400 --> 02:14:57.291
and, over time, leading where they choose
to. We’ll return to that shortly. First,

02:14:57.292 --> 02:15:02.063
keep asking who has heard this student’s
views and how those views travel to the

02:15:02.064 --> 02:15:04.177
other teachers and people involved.

02:15:05.680 --> 02:15:11.436
Turn to the Partnership activity grid on
page twenty-four. This gives us a way to

02:15:11.437 --> 02:15:17.264
look at the people in a partnership with a
little more care. Work through the four

02:15:17.265 --> 02:15:22.589
partners: the student, parents and carers,
subject teachers, and year level

02:15:22.590 --> 02:15:28.202
and support staff. For each, note what
they bring and the challenges they might

02:15:28.203 --> 02:15:32.951
face. Think about knowledge,
relationships, time, communication and

02:15:32.952 --> 02:15:38.492
the demands each person may be managing.
Avoid assuming that every person in a

02:15:38.493 --> 02:15:43.817
role has the same experience. Then compare
your four sections. What do they

02:15:43.818 --> 02:15:48.926
have in common? Where do you see different
knowledge or different needs?

02:15:48.927 --> 02:15:54.467
What could help them work together? Give
yourselves eight minutes for the full

02:15:54.468 --> 02:16:00.152
activity. Everyone can contribute in a way
that suits them, and you can write or

02:16:00.153 --> 02:16:05.981
reflect quietly as well as talk. Keep the
student at the centre of the grid. Pause

02:16:05.982 --> 02:16:08.930
the video now, and return for the debrief.

02:16:16.960 --> 02:16:21.596
Let's draw that together. Look across your
grid for similarities and

02:16:21.597 --> 02:16:26.718
differences. Which strengths might
complement one another? Which challenges

02:16:26.719 --> 02:16:31.770
could the partnership itself help address?
A useful next question is, what

02:16:31.771 --> 02:16:36.615
would make this partnership more
successful? Perhaps you need a clearer

02:16:36.616 --> 02:16:42.082
shared purpose, a different communication
arrangement, or a way for one person's

02:16:42.083 --> 02:16:47.481
knowledge to be heard earlier. Use the
circumstances you've identified to guide

02:16:47.482 --> 02:16:52.741
that choice. Return to our collaboration
principles: shared goals and values,

02:16:52.742 --> 02:16:57.516
trust and respect, consultation, curiosity
and student voice. Assuming

02:16:57.517 --> 02:17:02.084
good intent about outcomes for the student
can help us begin, while

02:17:02.085 --> 02:17:06.513
listening tells us what each person
actually needs and hopes for.

02:17:06.514 --> 02:17:11.981
Understanding each partner's strengths and
challenges gives trust and confidence

02:17:11.982 --> 02:17:14.265
something practical to grow from.

02:17:15.800 --> 02:17:20.714
Turn to The first two minutes on page
twenty-four. Find a partner. One person

02:17:20.715 --> 02:17:25.628
will be the teacher, and the other will
play a parent or a student. This is a

02:17:25.629 --> 02:17:30.414
low-stakes practice in asking and
listening back before offering solutions.

02:17:30.415 --> 02:17:35.134
Nobody needs to perform for the room.
Choose one of the three scenarios. A

02:17:35.135 --> 02:17:39.984
parent says their son in Year Eight has
come home upset every afternoon this

02:17:39.985 --> 02:17:44.770
week. He won’t say why, but he mentioned
Science. Another parent says their

02:17:44.771 --> 02:17:49.555
daughter has stopped handing in homework
and says she doesn’t know what the

02:17:49.556 --> 02:17:54.146
teachers want. Or a Year Ten student has
been invited to a meeting about

02:17:54.147 --> 02:17:59.449
assessment adjustments and is unsure they
want anyone to know. For two minutes, the

02:17:59.450 --> 02:18:04.493
teacher only asks questions and says back
what they have heard. Leave solutions

02:18:04.494 --> 02:18:09.343
aside. The scenarios are deliberately
incomplete, so stay curious about what

02:18:09.344 --> 02:18:14.387
you don’t know. If you would rather not
role-play, write what you would ask and

02:18:14.388 --> 02:18:19.496
say, then compare it with your partner.
Keep the pairs private and the parent or

02:18:19.497 --> 02:18:23.893
student role respectful. Decide who
starts. Pause the video and use a

02:18:23.894 --> 02:18:27.967
two-minute timer for this first round.
Then resume for the swap.

02:18:36.001 --> 02:18:41.195
Let’s stop there and swap roles. Choose a
second scenario from the page. The new

02:18:41.196 --> 02:18:46.587
teacher has the same task: ask and listen
back for two minutes, without moving into

02:18:46.588 --> 02:18:51.782
solutions yet. Notice the moments when you
want to offer an answer. Can you stay

02:18:51.783 --> 02:18:56.976
with a question a little longer and check
what the other person means? If you’re

02:18:56.977 --> 02:19:01.777
writing, try the second scenario or revise
your questions. Pause the video

02:19:01.778 --> 02:19:05.720
for the second two-minute round, then
return for the debrief.

02:19:13.761 --> 02:19:18.720
Let’s come back together. What did asking
first bring out? What made it hard to

02:19:18.721 --> 02:19:23.616
avoid jumping to a solution? Spend a
minute or two discussing those questions,

02:19:23.617 --> 02:19:28.513
or make a private note. Listening back
gives the student or family a chance to

02:19:28.514 --> 02:19:32.837
check what you understood. The
conversation may reveal a priority you

02:19:32.838 --> 02:19:37.670
would have missed if you started by
offering a strategy. That is something to

02:19:37.671 --> 02:19:42.375
listen for, rather than a result to
assume. Now write one question you will

02:19:42.376 --> 02:19:47.463
use to open a conversation in the space on
page twenty-four. Keep it with you for

02:19:47.464 --> 02:19:52.423
the bridge at the end of this session and
the conversation plan in Session Six.

02:19:52.424 --> 02:19:55.601
Pause here if you need longer to reflect
and write.

02:20:03.641 --> 02:20:07.867
Keep that opening question. We’ll build on
it as we consider how to make the

02:20:07.868 --> 02:20:11.812
whole consultation accessible, clear and
useful to the people involved.

02:20:13.320 --> 02:20:18.550
Meeting families where they are begins
with listening before solving. Ask about

02:20:18.551 --> 02:20:23.981
communication preferences and priorities.
Make room for questions and uncertainty,

02:20:23.982 --> 02:20:28.608
including questions we haven't
anticipated. Explain roles, options and

02:20:28.609 --> 02:20:33.839
next steps in plain language. Check what
is workable for the family now. A plan

02:20:33.840 --> 02:20:38.600
that looks simple within a school
timetable may interact with demands we

02:20:38.601 --> 02:20:44.032
don't know about. Avoid judging the pace
of a response, the choices someone makes,

02:20:44.033 --> 02:20:48.793
or the time they need to consider an
option. Recall masking from Session

02:20:48.794 --> 02:20:54.158
Three. Families may see a different part
of the experience after school or before

02:20:54.159 --> 02:20:58.986
the week begins. We need to listen to that
knowledge alongside what staff

02:20:58.987 --> 02:21:03.613
observe. Questions in advance, a phone
call, a different setting or an

02:21:03.614 --> 02:21:08.709
interpreter where needed may make it
easier to contribute. Check the person’s

02:21:08.710 --> 02:21:12.934
preferences. This brings our
environment-first thinking into the

02:21:12.935 --> 02:21:18.030
meeting itself. Where might the school's
language, timing or process create a

02:21:18.031 --> 02:21:22.926
barrier? What could we change? A different
format, questions in advance, a

02:21:22.927 --> 02:21:28.089
quieter place, or clearer follow-up may
change the experience of consultation.

02:21:28.090 --> 02:21:33.454
Keep asking what you need to understand.
Listening and clarity give us a stronger

02:21:33.455 --> 02:21:35.935
basis for solving something together.

02:21:37.440 --> 02:21:42.231
On page twenty-five you'll find the
Consultation checklist. You can use it

02:21:42.232 --> 02:21:46.958
before a meeting, and return to it
afterwards. Start with a clear, shared

02:21:46.959 --> 02:21:52.144
purpose. What are we trying to understand
or decide together? Use open questions

02:21:52.145 --> 02:21:57.133
that allow an answer you haven't already
chosen. Provide more than one way to

02:21:57.134 --> 02:22:02.253
contribute, and respect the knowledge the
student and family bring. Compare two

02:22:02.254 --> 02:22:07.308
possible questions: any concerns, and what
does a good morning look like? What

02:22:07.309 --> 02:22:11.969
might each bring out? A specific, open
question can make more room for a

02:22:11.970 --> 02:22:17.089
person’s experience. Record what you hear
accurately. It can help to check your

02:22:17.090 --> 02:22:22.275
understanding aloud: have I understood
that correctly? Then agree an action, who

02:22:22.276 --> 02:22:27.396
is responsible and how you will follow up.
If something remains undecided, make

02:22:27.397 --> 02:22:32.254
that clear too. Tell the student and
family what you did with the knowledge

02:22:32.255 --> 02:22:37.243
they shared, so they can see how it
influenced the next step. Afterwards, ask

02:22:37.244 --> 02:22:42.363
yourself whether the consultation changed
or extended your understanding. Could

02:22:42.364 --> 02:22:47.352
everyone contribute in a workable way? Do
people know what happens next? Ask,

02:22:47.353 --> 02:22:52.341
listen and follow up. A checklist or
planning tool can help us remember those

02:22:52.342 --> 02:22:56.543
steps, while the quality of the
consultation still depends on our

02:22:56.544 --> 02:22:58.709
attention to the people involved.

02:23:00.240 --> 02:23:06.063
Let's look first at consulting students.
Begin by presuming competence, and make

02:23:06.064 --> 02:23:11.370
communication accessible. That might
involve augmentative and alternative

02:23:11.371 --> 02:23:17.120
communication, visuals, writing, drawing,
video or symbols. Offer questions and

02:23:17.121 --> 02:23:22.280
meeting information beforehand, simple
choices where useful, and enough

02:23:22.281 --> 02:23:27.440
processing time. An adolescent might
prepare a written list, a one-page

02:23:27.441 --> 02:23:33.264
profile or a contribution with a trusted
adult who supports their communication.

02:23:33.265 --> 02:23:39.013
Notice the sensory environment. Build on
the student's strengths and interests.

02:23:39.014 --> 02:23:44.616
Behaviour may communicate preferences or
difficulty, so observe carefully and

02:23:44.617 --> 02:23:49.997
check your interpretation wherever
possible. Trusted supporters can assist

02:23:49.998 --> 02:23:55.894
communication while keeping the student's
own perspective central. What a student

02:23:55.895 --> 02:24:01.644
tells us needs to influence what we do.
With families, begin with the student's

02:24:01.645 --> 02:24:06.730
strengths, interests and what works.
Listen to their knowledge without

02:24:06.731 --> 02:24:11.890
assuming you already know their
experience. Use plain language, explain

02:24:11.891 --> 02:24:17.788
school terms and respect differences in
culture and perspective. Some parents may

02:24:17.789 --> 02:24:23.316
be neurodivergent themselves, so check
communication preferences rather than

02:24:23.317 --> 02:24:28.698
making assumptions. Use real examples from
school to discuss needs, goals,

02:24:28.699 --> 02:24:34.374
participation and possible adjustments.
Check shared understanding, agree next

02:24:34.375 --> 02:24:40.050
steps and keep the conversation open as
circumstances change. This extends the

02:24:40.051 --> 02:24:45.357
consultation work we introduced in Session
Four. Choose one practice from

02:24:45.358 --> 02:24:50.886
each column that you could try with the
student and family you have in mind.

02:24:50.887 --> 02:24:52.949
Pause to note those choices.

02:25:00.960 --> 02:25:05.421
As you look at your two choices, check
what they would make easier for the

02:25:05.422 --> 02:25:09.639
person involved. Can the student
communicate more comfortably? Can the

02:25:09.640 --> 02:25:14.406
family prepare, ask a question or add
knowledge that might otherwise be missed?

02:25:14.407 --> 02:25:19.296
Keep those choices with your conversation
notes. Consultation becomes more useful

02:25:19.297 --> 02:25:23.575
when we adapt it to the people involved,
and then act on what we learn.

02:25:25.080 --> 02:25:29.571
Adolescents can move from being talked
about to taking part in their own

02:25:29.572 --> 02:25:34.633
meetings, and over time taking more of a
lead. That needs preparation and support

02:25:34.634 --> 02:25:39.315
in a form that works for the young person.
Taking part might mean attending

02:25:39.316 --> 02:25:44.060
for part of the meeting, sending a written
contribution, preparing questions

02:25:44.061 --> 02:25:49.185
beforehand or using a one-page profile. A
trusted adult can support the student to

02:25:49.186 --> 02:25:54.183
communicate while keeping their own views
central. Being in the room is only one

02:25:54.184 --> 02:25:59.118
possible form of participation. A student
who chooses writing, a supporter or a

02:25:59.119 --> 02:26:04.116
shorter contribution is still expressing a
preference about how to take part. We

02:26:04.117 --> 02:26:08.988
need to respect that choice rather than
measure self-advocacy by how long they

02:26:08.989 --> 02:26:14.050
sit in a meeting. Think back to masking
and feeling safe. A young person may need

02:26:14.051 --> 02:26:18.478
a setting, time and preparation that allow
them to be themselves before

02:26:18.479 --> 02:26:23.224
sharing something difficult. Asking
directly, in an accessible way, gives us

02:26:23.225 --> 02:26:28.095
a better starting point than assuming what
they want. Practising these choices

02:26:28.096 --> 02:26:33.094
now can support the move towards senior
school, work and life after school. Keep

02:26:33.095 --> 02:26:38.155
the question on the slide with you: what
could the student have shaped? Where you

02:26:38.156 --> 02:26:42.772
can, ask the student directly and let what
you hear influence the meeting.

02:26:44.280 --> 02:26:49.385
Partnership becomes especially important
when a student’s capacity runs out. The

02:26:49.386 --> 02:26:54.104
terms on this slide are meltdown, shutdown
and what the slide calls School

02:26:54.105 --> 02:26:59.404
Can’t. That is, the student cannot attend
school. The workshop describes a meltdown

02:26:59.405 --> 02:27:04.640
as an outward response to overwhelm, and a
shutdown as an inward response that may

02:27:04.641 --> 02:27:09.164
be quiet and easily missed. It
distinguishes meltdown from a tantrum or

02:27:09.165 --> 02:27:14.206
a deliberate choice. Treating overwhelm as
wilful misbehaviour can lead us away

02:27:14.207 --> 02:27:19.183
from the support and understanding the
student needs. In secondary, a shutdown

02:27:19.184 --> 02:27:23.837
may be mistaken for disengagement or for
being good and chilled in class.

02:27:23.838 --> 02:27:28.814
Difficulty attending can also build over
time. Remember the question from this

02:27:28.815 --> 02:27:33.856
morning: what do we actually know about
this student’s experience? Ask what ran

02:27:33.857 --> 02:27:38.962
out and when. What demands accumulated
across the periods? What did each teacher

02:27:38.963 --> 02:27:43.810
see? What does the family notice after
school or before the next day begins?

02:27:43.811 --> 02:27:49.046
Different observations can help us build a
fuller picture together. Keep the focus

02:27:49.047 --> 02:27:53.958
on safety, what preceded the situation,
what supports recovery and what could

02:27:53.959 --> 02:27:58.806
change in the environment, task or
response. Use the relevant school support

02:27:58.807 --> 02:28:03.654
and safety processes. We are discussing
these experiences calmly and without

02:28:03.655 --> 02:28:08.566
blame, drawing on listening and the design
work from Session Four rather than

02:28:08.567 --> 02:28:13.026
trying to resolve an individual situation
from a workshop description.

02:28:14.560 --> 02:28:19.789
Here are two Positive Partnerships tools
that can help make those conversations

02:28:19.790 --> 02:28:24.817
concrete. On the left, the Working
Together Communication Tool asks how home

02:28:24.818 --> 02:28:30.047
and school will communicate, how often,
about what, and what the next steps and

02:28:30.048 --> 02:28:35.075
dates will be. You can discuss options
such as text, email, an app, phone, a

02:28:35.076 --> 02:28:39.970
communication book or face-to-face
conversation. Choose what is useful and

02:28:39.971 --> 02:28:44.998
workable together. On the right, the
Partnership Planning Template asks what

02:28:44.999 --> 02:28:50.295
has worked, what has been challenging and
what could help. It gives examples and

02:28:50.296 --> 02:28:55.257
space for your own ideas. You might use it
to explore meeting arrangements,

02:28:55.258 --> 02:29:00.755
feeling heard, communication or including
the young person's voice. For example, if

02:29:00.756 --> 02:29:05.716
a family says phone calls are hard to
manage during work, you could use the

02:29:05.717 --> 02:29:10.879
tools to discuss another option and agree
when it will be reviewed. That is an

02:29:10.880 --> 02:29:15.907
illustrative use of the prompts; the
family may suggest something different.

02:29:15.908 --> 02:29:21.070
Use the tools with people. Leave room for
their priorities to change what gets

02:29:21.071 --> 02:29:26.299
written. A completed page is useful when
it records a shared understanding that

02:29:26.300 --> 02:29:28.445
can guide the next conversation.

02:29:29.960 --> 02:29:35.271
The NCCD Meetings Toolkit is another
practical resource. NCCD stands for the

02:29:35.272 --> 02:29:40.866
Nationally Consistent Collection of Data
on School Students with Disability. You

02:29:40.867 --> 02:29:46.602
can find the toolkit on the official NCCD
website. Its sections support requesting

02:29:46.603 --> 02:29:51.985
and managing meetings, being prepared,
managing emotions, keeping records and

02:29:51.986 --> 02:29:57.226
considering whether a meeting was
successful. Use it to make the purpose of

02:29:57.227 --> 02:30:02.537
a meeting, the consultation process,
possible adjustments, evidence and next

02:30:02.538 --> 02:30:08.203
steps clear. Explain responsibilities and
follow-up in a way that the student and

02:30:08.204 --> 02:30:13.586
family can understand. People need to know
why a meeting is happening, who is

02:30:13.587 --> 02:30:19.181
involved and how they can contribute. The
student’s and family’s views, recorded

02:30:19.182 --> 02:30:24.917
accurately and acted on, also contribute
to the evidence about adjustments. Follow

02:30:24.918 --> 02:30:30.087
the processes used in your school or
system, and use current official NCCD

02:30:30.088 --> 02:30:35.328
information when you need specific
guidance. The toolkit gives you a way to

02:30:35.329 --> 02:30:40.640
prepare and review the process while
keeping the discussion connected to the

02:30:40.641 --> 02:30:42.127
student's experience.

02:30:43.640 --> 02:30:49.104
There are also practical support pathways
beyond today's workshop. Page thirty of

02:30:49.105 --> 02:30:54.159
your Participant Guide brings the
resources together. Positive Partnerships

02:30:54.160 --> 02:30:59.418
provides resources and learning options
for educators and families. This slide

02:30:59.419 --> 02:31:04.815
also shows Autism Connect, the national
autism information helpline, with phone,

02:31:04.816 --> 02:31:10.074
email and webchat options. Disability
Gateway can help people with disability,

02:31:10.075 --> 02:31:15.539
families and carers find information and
services. Carer Gateway provides support

02:31:15.540 --> 02:31:20.867
for carers. The contact details are on the
slide, so pause if you would like to

02:31:20.868 --> 02:31:25.990
record them. Choose a pathway that fits
the person's question, and check its

02:31:25.991 --> 02:31:31.250
website for the available support. We can
make families aware of these options

02:31:31.251 --> 02:31:34.119
while listening to what they want and
need.

02:31:37.120 --> 02:31:41.920
Before we finish this session, let's
prepare the bridge to one purposeful

02:31:41.921 --> 02:31:47.121
conversation. Return to the student you
have in mind and the lesson, assessment

02:31:47.122 --> 02:31:51.855
task or handover change on your My
students card. Open page twenty-five,

02:31:51.856 --> 02:31:57.056
Bridge to the next family conversation.
Work through these questions privately.

02:31:57.057 --> 02:32:01.924
What needs to be understood? What question
will open the conversation? How

02:32:01.925 --> 02:32:06.991
will the student take part, or have their
views included? What can be decided

02:32:06.992 --> 02:32:12.192
together? How and when will the school
follow up? The question you wrote during

02:32:12.193 --> 02:32:17.460
The first two minutes on page twenty-four
can become your opening question here.

02:32:17.461 --> 02:32:22.594
Bring in the process change you chose
during the power-dynamics activity. Make

02:32:22.595 --> 02:32:27.728
it part of how the conversation will
happen, as well as what you will ask. You

02:32:27.729 --> 02:32:32.596
are preparing your own purpose and
approach. Leave space for the student's

02:32:32.597 --> 02:32:37.530
and family's knowledge to change your
understanding and the next step. Keep

02:32:37.531 --> 02:32:42.665
names out of your workshop notes. Allow
about four minutes to write. Pause the

02:32:42.666 --> 02:32:46.732
video now, and resume when you're ready to
close this session.

02:32:54.760 --> 02:32:59.634
Take a final look at the question you plan
to open with. Does it invite the

02:32:59.635 --> 02:33:04.707
other person's knowledge? Have you left
something meaningful to understand and

02:33:04.708 --> 02:33:09.648
decide together? Keep these notes. In
Session Six, you'll use them to plan a

02:33:09.649 --> 02:33:14.786
real conversation on page twenty-eight,
alongside one design action. The person

02:33:14.787 --> 02:33:19.596
you named under who I will involve in
Session Four now has a place in that

02:33:19.597 --> 02:33:24.471
plan. We'll bring the day together through
belonging, capacity and joy, and

02:33:24.472 --> 02:33:29.609
choose the practical next steps you want
to carry back to school. Thank you for

02:33:29.610 --> 02:33:33.628
the care you've brought to thinking about
these relationships.

02:33:40.160 --> 02:33:45.786
Welcome to our final session, Flourishing
and Full Circle. We're going to return to

02:33:45.787 --> 02:33:51.001
the students and questions you began with,
and turn your thinking into a next

02:33:51.002 --> 02:33:56.354
step you can use. Have your My students
card and your partnership notes nearby.

02:33:56.355 --> 02:34:01.501
By the end of this session, you'll have
one design change and one purposeful

02:34:01.502 --> 02:34:06.716
conversation with a family, a student or a
colleague who teaches them to take

02:34:06.717 --> 02:34:11.657
forward. We'll also make time for
reflection, resources and feedback. You

02:34:11.658 --> 02:34:17.147
don't need to carry away an unmanageable
list. One thoughtful action, informed by

02:34:17.148 --> 02:34:21.676
the people involved and open to review, is
a useful place to begin.

02:34:23.200 --> 02:34:28.317
Belonging, capacity and joy bring the
threads of today together. Keep all three

02:34:28.318 --> 02:34:33.500
in the picture when you consider whether a
change is helping a student flourish.

02:34:33.501 --> 02:34:37.961
Belonging means being recognised, included
and able to participate as

02:34:37.962 --> 02:34:43.013
oneself. Capacity asks us to consider what
the day costs, what it protects and

02:34:43.014 --> 02:34:47.868
what helps restore energy. Joy includes
interests, connection and authentic

02:34:47.869 --> 02:34:52.395
expression. These ideas connect our
neurodiversity-affirming lens, our

02:34:52.396 --> 02:34:57.578
learning design across the secondary day
and our partnerships. We begin with the

02:34:57.579 --> 02:35:02.368
person, learn about their experience, and
bring student, family and school

02:35:02.369 --> 02:35:07.682
knowledge into the decisions. Look again
at the design change you chose in Session

02:35:07.683 --> 02:35:12.144
Four. It may reduce a barrier to
participation. Could it also support

02:35:12.145 --> 02:35:16.736
belonging or joy? How might it affect what
the day asks of the student?

02:35:16.737 --> 02:35:21.460
Remember the questions from Session Three:
what costs, and what lightens?

02:35:21.461 --> 02:35:26.709
Grades and attendance can be part of the
picture, but it won't tell us everything

02:35:26.710 --> 02:35:31.433
about a student's experience. Quiet
compliance or doing something without

02:35:31.434 --> 02:35:36.157
support is not, by itself, evidence of
flourishing. Keep returning to the

02:35:36.158 --> 02:35:41.143
student, and to belonging, capacity and
joy, as you judge what your change is

02:35:41.144 --> 02:35:42.193
making possible.

02:35:43.720 --> 02:35:48.839
Let's bring the strongest ideas together
at your table. Open page twenty-six of

02:35:48.840 --> 02:35:53.827
your Participant Guide. Each person has
about a minute to share the strongest

02:35:53.828 --> 02:35:58.619
thing they're taking back to school.
Choose something that has stayed with

02:35:58.620 --> 02:36:03.148
you: an insight, a question or a practical
change. You can draw on the

02:36:03.149 --> 02:36:08.268
gallery walk from Session Four as well as
your own planning. Then make a shared

02:36:08.269 --> 02:36:13.388
shortlist of three ideas from the table
that are most worth spreading. For each

02:36:13.389 --> 02:36:18.573
idea, name who needs to hear it, and the
meeting or moment where you could share

02:36:18.574 --> 02:36:23.365
it. Be as concrete as you can. Name the
actual faculty meeting, year level

02:36:23.366 --> 02:36:27.894
meeting or staff briefing, and the
colleague you need to speak with. A

02:36:27.895 --> 02:36:32.554
particular person and opportunity will
help you see what to do next. The

02:36:32.555 --> 02:36:37.674
activity allows eight minutes altogether.
If you're working independently, make

02:36:37.675 --> 02:36:42.925
your own shortlist and identify who you
could discuss it with. Pause here to work

02:36:42.926 --> 02:36:46.273
through the activity, then resume for the
next step.

02:36:54.280 --> 02:36:58.897
Let's return to your shortlist. Check that
each idea has somewhere to go: a

02:36:58.898 --> 02:37:03.640
person who needs to hear it, and a real
opportunity to talk. If an idea still

02:37:03.641 --> 02:37:08.569
feels broad, add one example of what it
could mean in practice. You can keep the

02:37:08.570 --> 02:37:13.000
other ideas for later. For now, we're
going to make your own next action

02:37:13.001 --> 02:37:16.245
specific enough to begin, and clear enough
to review.

02:37:17.760 --> 02:37:22.581
Turn to page twenty-seven, Start,
Continue, Reconsider. Place your Session

02:37:22.582 --> 02:37:27.932
Four commitment beside it, especially the
line, this week in my own classes. Under

02:37:27.933 --> 02:37:32.754
Start, choose a small strategy you can
begin next week. It can be the same

02:37:32.755 --> 02:37:37.642
this-week change you have already been
developing. Under Continue, identify

02:37:37.643 --> 02:37:42.597
something today has confirmed you already
do well. Under Reconsider, name an

02:37:42.598 --> 02:37:47.155
approach you would now look at
differently. For each, ask how you will

02:37:47.156 --> 02:37:52.043
know it worked, and what evidence will
help you judge that. For your Start,

02:37:52.044 --> 02:37:57.262
these sentence stems may help: I will. The
barrier or demand I am addressing is.

02:37:57.263 --> 02:38:02.480
I will notice. I need to consult. I will
review this on. Choose something within

02:38:02.481 --> 02:38:07.236
your influence. Keep the focus on the
student's access, participation and

02:38:07.237 --> 02:38:12.389
experience. An action that depends on the
student appearing less Autistic needs

02:38:12.390 --> 02:38:17.740
rethinking. Consider what you can change
in the environment, task or response, and

02:38:17.741 --> 02:38:23.024
whose knowledge you need before or while
you try it. Allow eight minutes. This is

02:38:23.025 --> 02:38:28.177
your card. When you're ready, share one
thing with someone at your table if you

02:38:28.178 --> 02:38:33.130
feel comfortable. Pause the video now, and
we'll return to check the action.

02:38:41.160 --> 02:38:46.414
Let's look once more at your Start. Is it
small enough to begin? Does it name a

02:38:46.415 --> 02:38:51.534
barrier or demand you can act on? Have you
identified when you'll consult the

02:38:51.535 --> 02:38:56.856
student, family or team, and when you'll
review what happens? Check the evidence

02:38:56.857 --> 02:39:01.976
you plan to notice. If the measure is
simply that the student becomes quieter

02:39:01.977 --> 02:39:06.826
or complies more readily, look again. What
will help you understand their

02:39:06.827 --> 02:39:11.947
access, participation and experience?
Include their perspective in a way that

02:39:11.948 --> 02:39:16.864
works for them. Keep the action small,
testable and open to revision. Your

02:39:16.865 --> 02:39:21.782
Continue and Reconsider are useful
alongside it: one recognises a practice

02:39:21.783 --> 02:39:25.555
worth sustaining, and the other keeps your
learning open.

02:39:27.080 --> 02:39:32.167
Now let's plan the next conversation, on
page twenty-eight. Bring forward your

02:39:32.168 --> 02:39:37.056
Session Five bridge notes from page
twenty-five, and the process change you

02:39:37.057 --> 02:39:42.012
chose in the power-dynamics reflection on
page twenty-three. Choose one real

02:39:42.013 --> 02:39:46.703
conversation you can have within two
weeks, with a student’s family, the

02:39:46.704 --> 02:39:51.790
student themselves, or a colleague who
teaches them. Where you can, choose the

02:39:51.791 --> 02:39:57.010
family or student conversation so their
knowledge can shape the work directly. A

02:39:57.011 --> 02:40:02.296
colleague handover is also a real choice
in secondary: keeping support consistent

02:40:02.297 --> 02:40:07.186
across teachers is part of partnership.
Begin by writing the purpose in one

02:40:07.187 --> 02:40:12.604
sentence. What do you need to understand?
Leave room for their perspective to shape

02:40:12.605 --> 02:40:17.823
the conversation. Plan what you'll share,
beginning with a strength, what you'll

02:40:17.824 --> 02:40:23.043
ask specifically, and what you'll offer.
Include how the student's communication

02:40:23.044 --> 02:40:28.197
and preferences will be heard. Write two
open questions, and note what you will

02:40:28.198 --> 02:40:32.822
listen for. If you’re planning a handover,
return to what should travel

02:40:32.823 --> 02:40:38.108
between people and places on page eleven.
How will the student’s own voice travel

02:40:38.109 --> 02:40:43.130
too? A conversation about them still needs
their perspective in it. Now write

02:40:43.131 --> 02:40:48.548
your opening sentence, word for word. You
can draw on the opening question from The

02:40:48.549 --> 02:40:53.701
first two minutes on page twenty-four. Say
it quietly to yourself if that helps

02:40:53.702 --> 02:40:58.921
you hear how it might land. Is it clear
and respectful? Does it invite something

02:40:58.922 --> 02:41:04.141
you don't already know? Name one possible
shared next step, leaving room for the

02:41:04.142 --> 02:41:09.295
conversation to change it. Record how
you'll follow up, and when, where and how

02:41:09.296 --> 02:41:14.647
the conversation will happen. Apply your
process change to those arrangements. Use

02:41:14.648 --> 02:41:19.668
initials or roles in your workshop notes.
You have eight minutes for the full

02:41:19.669 --> 02:41:23.038
activity. Pause here to make your plan,
then resume.

02:41:31.040 --> 02:41:36.360
Take a final look at your conversation
plan. Have you prepared to listen, as

02:41:36.361 --> 02:41:41.823
well as to speak? Does the plan leave
something meaningful to decide together?

02:41:41.824 --> 02:41:47.216
Have you made a way for the student's own
perspective to be included? You can

02:41:47.217 --> 02:41:52.537
prepare your opening and your questions
without scripting the other person's

02:41:52.538 --> 02:41:57.361
response. If new knowledge changes the
next step you had in mind, the

02:41:57.362 --> 02:42:02.824
conversation is doing useful work. Keep
the time and follow-up visible in your

02:42:02.825 --> 02:42:07.578
plan. One design action and one
conversation now connect what you've

02:42:07.579 --> 02:42:11.906
learned today to the people and setting
where you will use it.

02:42:13.440 --> 02:42:18.196
This slide is the place for Caitlin's
account of how teacher belief shaped

02:42:18.197 --> 02:42:23.280
their success. That video hasn't been
supplied in this version of the workshop,

02:42:23.281 --> 02:42:27.972
so we'll move directly to the final
reflection. Carry its focus with you:

02:42:27.973 --> 02:42:32.990
teacher belief, student experience, and
the difference our responses can make.

02:42:34.520 --> 02:42:39.499
Return to page five of your Participant
Guide, My starting point. Earlier, you

02:42:39.500 --> 02:42:44.415
wrote about what school is for and what
you were curious about. Read your own

02:42:44.416 --> 02:42:48.943
words back. Now turn to the final
reflection on page twenty-nine. Where

02:42:48.944 --> 02:42:53.600
were you when we began? Where are you now?
What's different in how you're

02:42:53.601 --> 02:42:58.516
thinking about the students you have in
mind? You may have more questions now

02:42:58.517 --> 02:43:03.690
than when you began. Recognising what you
still need to understand can be part of

02:43:03.691 --> 02:43:08.541
your learning. Take a little quiet time
with those questions. Then write one

02:43:08.542 --> 02:43:13.651
sentence, just for yourself: what will you
do differently in your teaching? This

02:43:13.652 --> 02:43:18.502
reflection can stay private. There is no
need to share it. The live workshop

02:43:18.503 --> 02:43:23.676
allows four minutes here. Pause the video
for the time you need, then resume when

02:43:23.677 --> 02:43:24.581
you are ready.

02:43:32.600 --> 02:43:36.935
Keep that sentence with your action card.
It may capture a shift you want to

02:43:36.936 --> 02:43:41.559
remember when you're back in the pace of a
school day. You can return to it after

02:43:41.560 --> 02:43:45.779
you've tried your change and had your
conversation, and notice what you've

02:43:45.780 --> 02:43:46.530
learned then.

02:43:48.040 --> 02:43:53.372
What are you curious to learn more about
next? Look back at your questions on page

02:43:53.373 --> 02:43:58.574
five. Which have been answered, and which
have become more specific? Page thirty

02:43:58.575 --> 02:44:03.314
brings together pathways for continuing
the learning. Webinars and deeper

02:44:03.315 --> 02:44:08.187
sessions can help you explore topics such
as Universal Design for Learning,

02:44:08.188 --> 02:44:13.586
executive functioning, sensory design and
anxiety. The Online Community of Practice

02:44:13.587 --> 02:44:18.326
is intended as a place to keep talking
with other educators. The Positive

02:44:18.327 --> 02:44:23.001
Partnerships website also provides
resources for educators, families and

02:44:23.002 --> 02:44:28.203
students. The particular webinar titles
and dates, and the Community of Practice

02:44:28.204 --> 02:44:33.075
link, are still awaiting confirmation in
these materials. Use the confirmed

02:44:33.076 --> 02:44:37.816
follow-up information from your workshop
organiser when those details are

02:44:37.817 --> 02:44:42.688
available. The workshop plan also includes
sharing photographs of wall work

02:44:42.689 --> 02:44:47.692
through that community; check the local
arrangements for your group. For now,

02:44:47.693 --> 02:44:52.301
choose the pathway that fits your next
question. You can begin with the

02:44:52.302 --> 02:44:57.502
resources at positive partnerships dot com
dot au. Today is a starting point for

02:44:57.503 --> 02:45:00.531
continued learning, reflection and
partnership.

02:45:02.040 --> 02:45:06.895
Here are the Positive Partnerships website
and social channels. You can note

02:45:06.896 --> 02:45:12.075
the details, take a screenshot, or return
to this slide later. The slide includes

02:45:12.076 --> 02:45:16.997
Facebook, Instagram, Threads, LinkedIn and
Pinterest, as well as the website.

02:45:16.998 --> 02:45:21.982
Choose the way of staying connected that
works for you. Pause the video if you

02:45:21.983 --> 02:45:24.701
would like longer with the contact
details.

02:45:27.720 --> 02:45:32.613
Before we close, there is time for
feedback. Your reflections can help the

02:45:32.614 --> 02:45:37.440
team understand what was useful, what
needs clarification, and what could

02:45:37.441 --> 02:45:42.535
improve the workshop experience. The
feedback link is on this slide, with the

02:45:42.536 --> 02:45:47.429
code you can scan. If that way of
accessing the form doesn't work for you,

02:45:47.430 --> 02:45:52.726
let your workshop organiser know so you
can discuss an accessible way to provide

02:45:52.727 --> 02:45:58.089
your feedback. The live workshop protects
four minutes for this. If your group is

02:45:58.090 --> 02:46:03.050
using the form, pause the video now and
take that time. Then resume for our

02:46:03.051 --> 02:46:03.989
closing words.

02:46:12.000 --> 02:46:16.719
Thank you for taking the time to reflect
on the workshop. Keep any questions you

02:46:16.720 --> 02:46:21.439
still want to explore alongside your next
steps, so they can inform the learning

02:46:21.440 --> 02:46:23.051
that continues after today.

02:46:24.560 --> 02:46:28.966
Thank you for your attention, questions
and professional reflection throughout

02:46:28.967 --> 02:46:33.488
these sessions. Take your action card and
conversation plan with you, along with

02:46:33.489 --> 02:46:37.495
the sentence you wrote in your final
reflection. Stay curious, and keep

02:46:37.496 --> 02:46:41.616
students at the centre. Take one
thoughtful next step, include the people

02:46:41.617 --> 02:46:46.138
who hold relevant knowledge, and review
what happens. Student voice, observation

02:46:46.139 --> 02:46:50.545
and partnership can help you refine a
small change into something that makes a

02:46:50.546 --> 02:46:55.124
meaningful difference. Use the resources
and the confirmed follow-up arrangements

02:46:55.125 --> 02:46:59.131
from your workshop team to stay connected.
You don't need to have every

02:46:59.132 --> 02:47:03.710
answer before you begin listening, asking
a better question or changing something

02:47:03.711 --> 02:47:08.002
within your influence. Good luck with your
next steps, and thank you for the

02:47:08.003 --> 02:47:09.605
care you bring to this work.

